{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat research/README.md && rg -n '\"'^#{1,4} '\"' docs/01-landscape.md docs/02-product-spec.md docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Axylo landscape research\n\nEvidence behind [docs/01-landscape.md](../docs/01-landscape.md). Collected 2026-11-17, with probes added and rerun on 2026-11-18 after review ([ledger](../reviews/2026-11-18/ledger.md)).\n\n## adoption/\n\n| File | What it is | How to refresh |\n|---|---|---|\n| `adoption.json` | npm weekly and yearly downloads, registry metadata, jsDelivr monthly hits and GitHub stars for about fifty charting packages | `node fetch.mjs > adoption.json && node refill.mjs` |\n| `httparchive.json` | Number of origins on which the HTTP Archive crawl detected each charting technology | `node httparchive.mjs > httparchive.json` |\n| `cadence.json` | Major-version dates and stable release counts for the ten studied libraries | `node cadence.mjs` |\n| `ranks.json` | Each candidate's rank on every adoption channel, and what each channel alone would select | `node ranks.mjs` |\n\nThe npm downloads API rate-limits bursts; `refill.mjs` retries the packages that came back empty.\n\n## harness/\n\nOne page that runs the same tasks through ten libraries, and a probe that drives system Chrome through all of them.\n\n```bash\ncd harness\nnpm ci\nnpx vite build\nnpx vite preview --port 4173 --strictPort   # leave running\nnode probe.mjs                              # look, empty, a11y, env, perf\nnode probe.mjs dates sentinels lightonly    # added after review\nLIBS=highcharts-plain,echarts-aria node probe.mjs look a11y\nLIBS=noop,chartjs-tuned,echarts-tuned,recharts-tuned,highcharts-tuned,plotly-tuned node probe.mjs perf\nnode montage.mjs trend bars parts dirty empty kbd dark resize lightonly\nnode palette.mjs\nnode spot-checks.mjs\nnode sizes.mjs\n```\n\n| Path | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| `src/data.js` | The shared datasets. Every library receives exactly these values. |\n| `src/libs/<library>.js(x)` | The least configuration each library needs for each task. Comments mark what was required beyond data. |\n| `src/libs/<library>-tuned.js(x)` | The same large-data task in a tuned configuration. These change several options at once, some of them visible to the reader. |\n| `src/libs/highcharts-plain.js`, `echarts-aria.js` | Each library's accessibility switch in the other position. |\n| `src/libs/noop.js` | Draws nothing: the measurement floor. |\n| `probe.mjs` | DOM and accessibility-tree capture, keyboard walk, pointer hover in three time zones for instants and for date strings, one bad value at a time, resize, dark scheme, a light-only page on a dark device, motion with and without reduced-motion, timings and browser-reported main-thread time. |\n| `results.json` | Probe output, merged across runs. `_runs` lists every invocation since the log was added; results from the first pass predate it. |\n| `spot-checks.json` | Small checks the report quotes: the WebGL renderer in headless Chrome, which packages mention the reduced-motion media query, the ECharts and MUI X accessibility switches. |\n| `perf.log`, `rerun.log` | Console output of the first and second performance passes. The report uses the second. |\n| `shots/` | Screenshots; `_sheet-*.png` are the side-by-side sheets. |\n| `palette.json`, `sizes.json` | Default palette scores and bundle sizes. |\n\nOpen one case by hand at `http://localhost:4173/?lib=highcharts&task=trend`. Tasks: `trend`, `dates`, `bars`, `parts`, `dirty`, `dirty-null`, `dirty-nan`, `dirty-string`, `dirty-undefined`, `empty`, `scale&n=100000`, `stream`. Add `&scheme=light` for a light-only page.\n\n## palette/\n\n`node search.mjs` searches for 6- and 8-color categorical palettes that reach 3:1 contrast on light and dark backgrounds while staying apart under simulated color-vision deficiencies. `feasibility.json` holds the result. It shows which thresholds are reachable; it is not Axylo's palette.\n\n## tables.mjs\n\n`node tables.mjs` prints the Markdown tables in the landscape report from the JSON files above.\n\n### Reading the timings\n\nTimings are from headless Chrome on one desktop machine, three runs each, and are for ranking by order of magnitude. The report uses main-thread task time as the browser reports it (through the DevTools protocol), which counts work however a library schedules it but not rasterization on other threads. The older \"time to second frame\" figure is still recorded as `toPaintMs`; it cannot resolve below about 35 ms and stops counting before entry animations finish. The streaming producer sends one update per animation frame, so it is not an independent 60 Hz feed. WebGL ran on a software rasterizer. The performance plan replaces all of this with a stricter method ([docs/04-plan-performance.md](../docs/04-plan-performance.md)).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:1:# Phase 2 plan: Axylo 1.0 in TypeScript\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:9:## 1. Public API\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:13:### 1.1 Shape\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:38:### 1.2 Data: one logical table, three forms\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:63:### 1.3 Fields\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:90:### 1.4 Common options\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:107:### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:130:### 1.6 Diagnostics\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:149:### 1.7 React adapter (not in 1.0)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:169:## 2. Architecture\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:171:### 2.1 Pipeline\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:192:### 2.2 Invalidation\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:206:### 2.3 Rendering\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:218:### 2.4 Document structure\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:241:### 2.5 Repository\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:257:## 3. Milestones\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:261:### M0. Foundations (ceiling: 5 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:270:### M1. A vertical slice, and the two open decisions (ceiling: 12 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:286:### M2. Faithful data, on the line chart (ceiling: 8 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:298:### M3. Reading the chart (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:311:### M4. Accessibility, complete on the line chart (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:324:### M5. Bar, area, donut, scatter and the compact variant (ceiling: 18 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:338:### M6. Updates and scale (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:350:### M7. Distribution and documentation (ceiling: 8 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:361:### M8. Release (ceiling: 10 days)\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:372:## 4. Test strategy\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:388:## 5. Risks\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:400:## 6. What waits until after 1.0\ndocs/01-landscape.md:1:# Phase 1a: the browser charting landscape, November 2026\ndocs/01-landscape.md:5:## Summary\ndocs/01-landscape.md:24:## 1. Which libraries, and how sure can we be\ndocs/01-landscape.md:26:### 1.1 Adoption data\ndocs/01-landscape.md:57:### 1.2 What these numbers cannot tell us\ndocs/01-landscape.md:71:### 1.3 The ten: a chosen sample, not a ranking\ndocs/01-landscape.md:95:### 1.4 Maintenance and cost facts\ndocs/01-landscape.md:112:## 2. Method\ndocs/01-landscape.md:142:## 3. How the charts look and communicate\ndocs/01-landscape.md:146:### 3.1 Scoreboard\ndocs/01-landscape.md:162:### 3.2 Findings\ndocs/01-landscape.md:248:## 4. How readers explore and interact\ndocs/01-landscape.md:250:### 4.1 Scoreboard\ndocs/01-landscape.md:268:### 4.2 Findings\ndocs/01-landscape.md:290:### 4.3 Accessibility with each library's switch off and on\ndocs/01-landscape.md:304:## 5. How developers create, update and maintain\ndocs/01-landscape.md:308:### 5.1 What each library needed beyond data\ndocs/01-landscape.md:325:### 5.2 Findings\ndocs/01-landscape.md:341:### 5.3 Load\ndocs/01-landscape.md:384:## 6. What the findings add up to\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:1:# Phase 1b: Axylo product specification\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:7:## 1. What Axylo is\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:15:### 1.1 Why someone would choose it\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:29:### 1.2 Who it is for\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:35:### 1.3 Principles\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:46:## 2. Scope of the first release\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:48:### 2.1 Chart types\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:65:### 2.2 Workflows\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:76:### 2.3 Supported environments\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:87:## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:91:### 3.1 Faithful representation\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:108:### 3.2 Legibility\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:121:### 3.3 Interaction\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:132:### 3.4 Accessibility\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:149:### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:189:### 3.6 Type matrix\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:201:### 3.7 Compact variant\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:215:### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:219:## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:236:## 5. Performance budgets\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:257:## 6. Decisions and the findings behind them\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:282:## 7. Changes this revision rejected\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:291:## 8. Decisions still open\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
