{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '112,383p' docs/01-landscape.md\"","aggregated_output":"## 2. Method\n\nOne Vite project loads each library from npm and runs the same tasks into a 720×400 container. A Playwright script drives system Chrome 157 (headless) and records screenshots, the DOM, the accessibility tree, keyboard and pointer behavior, and timings.\n\n**Rule for configuration.** Each task states what the reader must be able to do: see the data with axes and a title, tell series apart, read exact values. Each library gets the least configuration its documentation needs for that, and no styling. This is \"minimum to complete the task\", not the bare default: ECharts is given its tooltip and legend, and Highcharts its accessibility module, because their documentation presents those as the normal setup. Where a library cannot draw without a choice (a color, a size), the value from its own documentation is used. Section 4.3 reports the accessibility results with each library's switch off and on, since the first version compared one library's opt-in state with nine defaults.\n\n| Task | Data | What it tests |\n|---|---|---|\n| `trend` | 3 series × 120 daily values as instants (epoch milliseconds at UTC midnight); one series has 7 missing days, so 353 finite values | Time axis, legend, tooltip, number formatting, gaps |\n| `dates` | The same series keyed by date strings (`2026-02-24`) | Whether a date stays a date |\n| `bars` | 7 categories, values 82–97, one long category name | Zero baseline, label fitting |\n| `parts` | 6 shares, two under 3% | Part-to-whole labels, palette |\n| `dirty` | `[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]` | Bad input, combined |\n| `dirty-null`, `-nan`, `-string`, `-undefined` | `[10, 20, 30, X, 50, 60, 70]` with one bad value | Bad input, one kind at a time |\n| `empty` | The `trend` chart with zero rows | Empty state |\n| `scale` | One series of 10,000, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points | Cost to draw |\n| `stream` | 600-point window, one new point per animation frame for 300 frames | Cost to update |\n\nProbes on `trend`: Tab, then Right, Right, Down, Enter from the first stop; pointer moved into the plot with the browser in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata; container shrunk from 720 to 360 px; container created at 340 px inside an 800 px viewport; `prefers-color-scheme: dark` on a page that follows it, and on a page that declares itself light-only. On `bars`: first frame against the frame 2.5 s later, with and without `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n\n**What this method cannot show.**\n\n- Chrome only. No Firefox, no Safari, no touch, no real phone viewport.\n- No screen reader was run. \"Announced\" below means text appeared in a live region or an accessible name, not that anything was heard.\n- The keyboard walk tries arrows only from the first tab stop, so a library whose point navigation starts elsewhere would be missed.\n- The motion probe compares two screenshots of one chart type; \"ignores reduced motion\" means the bar entry transition specifically. No update was tested.\n- Screenshots are cropped to the chart container, so clipping at the container edge and overflow onto the page look the same.\n- No task customizes anything: no brand colors, fonts or formatters. The brief asked how developers customize charts; that part of the comparison was not done.\n- Every library has options that change most of what follows. The study records what a reader gets when the author does the minimum.\n\n## 3. How the charts look and communicate\n\n![Trend task](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-trend.png)\n\n### 3.1 Scoreboard\n\nAll cells are **Observed** unless marked.\n\n| | Chart.js | Recharts | D3 (authored) | ECharts | Highcharts | ApexCharts | Plotly.js | Nivo | MUI X | uPlot |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bars start at zero | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | no: starts at 80 (bar path builder with the default y range) |\n| Long category label | all labels rotated | **label dropped** | overlaps | **5 of 7 dropped** | wrapped | rotated, cut at the container edge | rotated, cut at the container edge | overlaps | **label dropped** | overlaps |\n| Time tick labels at 720 px | rotated 45° | crowded | fit | overlap | fit | fit | fit | overlap | fit | fit |\n| Thousands on the y axis | `50,000` | `60000` | `50,000` | `50,000` | `60k` | `50000` | `50k` | `50000` | `50,0…` (cut) | `50,000` |\n| Marker on every point of a 120-point line | yes | yes | no | yes | no | no | no | yes | no | no |\n| Missing days shown as a gap | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes |\n| Visible chart title | built in | none | authored | built in | built in | built in | built in | none | none (accessible `title` only) | built in |\n| Donut: slice values readable without hovering | 0 of 6 | 6 of 6 (absent 1.8 s after creation, present at 4 s) | 4 of 6 (authored) | 0 of 6 (names only) | 0 of 6 (names only) | 4 of 6 (percent) | 6 of 6 (percent) | 6 of 6, two overlap | 0 of 6 | no donut |\n| Empty data | invented axes (0–1.0, hours) | blank frame | blank frame | blank | blank | invented axis (0–6) | invented axes (−1 to 6) | \"Jan 01\" eight times | **\"No data to display\"** | blank |\n\n### 3.2 Findings\n\n**R1. Zero baseline.** Nine of ten start bars at zero. uPlot has no bar chart type; with its bar path builder and the default y range, bars started at 80. That is a configuration a uPlot author would be expected to complete, and a place where a default can mislead.\n\n**R2. Labels that do not fit are the most common visible failure.** Only Highcharts wrapped \"Customer Success & Onboarding\". Recharts and MUI X removed that label and left a bar with no name; ECharts kept two labels of seven. Nivo, uPlot and the authored D3 chart overlapped text. ApexCharts and Plotly rotated the labels and they were cut at the container edge. Chart.js rotated all labels, and they stayed complete and readable.\n\n![Bars task](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-bars.png)\n\n**R3. Instants and dates are not told apart.** The pointer was moved to the same place with the browser in three zones. The cell shows the date each library displayed.\n\nGiven instants (epoch milliseconds at UTC midnight):\n\n| Library | Reader in UTC | Los Angeles | Kolkata | Same day everywhere |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Chart.js | Feb 24, 12:00 a.m. | Feb 23, 4:00 p.m. | Feb 24, 5:30 a.m. | no |\n| ECharts | 2026-02-26 00:00 | 2026-02-25 16:00 | 2026-02-26 05:30 | no |\n| Plotly.js | Mar 10 | Mar 9 | Mar 10 | no |\n| Nivo | Sat Mar 07 00:00 | Fri Mar 06 16:00 | Sat Mar 07 05:30 | no |\n| MUI X Charts | Mon Feb 23 00:00 | Sun Feb 22 16:00 | Mon Feb 23 05:30 | no |\n| uPlot | 02-24 12:00am | 02-23 4:00pm | 02-24 5:30am | no |\n| Highcharts | Feb 24 | Feb 24 | Feb 24 | yes (UTC by default) |\n| ApexCharts | 24 Feb | 24 Feb | 24 Feb | yes (UTC by default) |\n| Recharts | decided by the author's formatter; the harness formatter showed the local date | | | – |\n| D3 (authored) | UTC scale chosen in the code | | | – |\n\nGiven date strings:\n\n| Same day in all three zones | Day still depends on the zone |\n|---|---|\n| Chart.js, ECharts, Highcharts, ApexCharts, Plotly.js; Recharts (prints the string on a category axis); D3 (authored) | Nivo (its default tooltip prints a `Date` in local time); MUI X and uPlot (they accept only `Date` objects or numbers, so the string has to be converted first) |\n\nShowing 4:00 p.m. on the previous day is a faithful rendering of an instant. It is wrong only if the author meant a calendar date, and common back ends deliver calendar dates as UTC-midnight instants (a `Date` serialized to JSON, for example). **Inferred**: the gap is not that libraries shift dates, but that none of the ten asks or signals which kind of value it was given.\n\nThe Chart.js tooltip is drawn on canvas; its text was read through the library's tooltip API for one known datum and is saved in `results.json` under `chartjs.dates`.\n\n**R4. Default value formatting is uneven.** Recharts, ApexCharts and Nivo print `50000` on the axis. Nivo's default tooltip is `x: Sat Mar 07 2026 00:00:00 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time), y: 24930`, and MUI X's tooltip heading is the same raw date string. Plotly abbreviates the tooltip value to `36.832k`, which is exact here but not how a reader would write 36,832. ECharts' generated accessibility description (section 4.3) reads out timestamps as `1767225600000`.\n\n**R5. Bad input is not reported.** Outcomes with one bad value among six valid ones:\n\n| | `null` | `NaN` | `'40'` (a string) | `undefined` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Chart.js | gap | gap | plotted as 40 | gap |\n| Recharts | gap | gap | plotted as 40 | gap |\n| ECharts | gap | gap | plotted as 40 | gap |\n| Plotly.js | gap | gap | plotted as 40 | gap |\n| Highcharts | gap | gap | gap | **throws** `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'x')`; empty chart |\n| ApexCharts | gap | **whole series missing**, no message | plotted as 40 | **whole series missing**, no message |\n| Nivo | gap | **chart broken**, 14 console errors | plotted as 40 | **chart broken**, 14 console errors |\n| MUI X Charts | gap | **chart broken**, 21 console errors | plotted as 40 | gap |\n| uPlot | gap | **line drawn across** | plotted as 40 | line drawn across (documented: `undefined` means \"join\") |\n| D3 (authored, `.defined()` omitted on purpose) | line stops | line stops | plotted as 40 | line stops |\n\nEvery library handled `null` as a gap. Nine plotted a numeric string as a number, and Highcharts silently left it out; none said so either way. The only messages were one exception from minified code and browser SVG attribute errors. No library named the field, the row or the problem. Whether plotting `'40'` is wrong depends on the data (database drivers return large counts as strings); the finding is that the author is not told that a guess was made.\n\n![One bad value at a time](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-one-bad-value.png)\n\n**R6. Empty results look broken.** Only MUI X says \"No data to display\". Chart.js, ApexCharts, Plotly and Nivo draw axes with invented ranges; the rest are blank. Highcharts' generated description for the empty chart reads \"Data ranges from 1970-01-01 00:00:00 to 1970-01-01 00:00:00\".\n\n**R7. Default palettes fail contrast, color-vision checks, or both.** The colors of the six donut slices, in order, scored for contrast against white (WCAG 1.4.11 asks 3:1 for graphical objects that are needed to understand the content) and for the smallest pairwise difference in OKLab under normal vision and Machado-simulated deficiencies. Repeated colors count as a pair with zero difference.\n\n| Library | Colors of the six slices | Distinct | Below 3:1 on white | Closest pair: normal | Protanopia | Deuteranopia | Tritanopia |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Chart.js | `#36a2eb` `#ff6384` `#ff9f40` `#ffcd56` `#4bc0c0` `#9966ff` | 6 | 5 | 0.108 | 0.114 | 0.084 | 0.051 |\n| Recharts (documentation colors; it has no palette) | `#0088fe` `#00c49f` `#ffbb28` `#ff8042` `#0088fe` `#00c49f` | 4 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| ECharts | `#5070dd` `#b6d634` `#505372` `#ff994d` `#0ca8df` `#ffd10a` | 6 | 4 | 0.100 | 0.016 | 0.047 | 0.092 |\n| Highcharts | `#2caffe` `#544fc5` `#00e272` `#fe6a35` `#6b8abc` `#d568fb` | 6 | 4 | 0.123 | 0.107 | 0.012 | 0.082 |\n| ApexCharts | `#008ffb` `#00a86f` `#ca8501` `#ff4560` `#846dd5` `#008ffb` | 5 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| Plotly.js | `#1f77b4` `#ff7f0e` `#2ca02c` `#d62728` `#9467bd` `#8c564b` | 6 | 1 | 0.142 | 0.007 | 0.039 | 0.068 |\n| Nivo | `#e8c1a0` `#f47560` `#f1e15b` `#e8a838` `#61cdbb` `#97e3d5` | 6 | 6 | 0.087 | 0.040 | 0.069 | 0.065 |\n| MUI X Charts | `#4254fb` `#ffb422` `#fa4f58` `#0dbeff` `#22bf75` `#fa83b4` | 6 | 4 | 0.131 | 0.081 | 0.046 | 0.067 |\n\nApexCharts is the only one whose colors all reach 3:1, and its sixth slice has the same blue as its first, next to it on the ring. Recharts has no palette (every line is `#3182bd` and every sector `#808080` until the author assigns colors); the repeat above comes from cycling the four colors its examples use, which is **authored**. uPlot has no palette. Plotly's orange and green are nearly identical under simulated protanopia (0.007). ECharts was scored from its option palette, not from rendered pixels.\n\nA search under the same metric found eight-color sets that reach 3:1 and keep a minimum difference of 0.12 on white and 0.14 on a dark background ([`research/palette/feasibility.json`](../research/palette/feasibility.json)). Those sets cover few hues, differ between light and dark, and were scored as swatches, not as 2 px lines. So the thresholds are reachable; whether a palette people would choose meets them is open.\n\n![Donut task](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-parts.png)\n\n**R8. Dense lines get a marker on every point in four libraries.** Chart.js, Recharts, ECharts and Nivo draw one for each of the 353 values; at 340 px the lines turn into beads.\n\n**R9. Titles and units are thin in the React libraries.** Recharts and Nivo have no chart title. MUI X has a `title` prop that sets an accessible label only. In Recharts and MUI X the y-axis title overlapped the tick labels with the documented placement. Highcharts titles an untitled y axis \"Values\".\n\n**R10. Following the page's color scheme is rare, and following the device is not the same thing.** On a page that follows `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, two charts stayed legible without configuration: Highcharts 13, which switches palette and text, and the authored D3 chart, whose axis text uses the inherited color. On a page that declares itself light-only, viewed on a device set to dark, Highcharts drew a black chart on the white page: it follows the device preference, not the page. The other nine stayed light there. **Inferred**: a chart should take its scheme from the page it is in.\n\n![Dark page](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-dark.png)\n![Light-only page on a dark device](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-lightonly.png)\n\n## 4. How readers explore and interact\n\n### 4.1 Scoreboard\n\nAll cells are **Observed** in Chrome's accessibility tree and by scripted keyboard and pointer input. No screen reader was run.\n\n| | Chart.js | Recharts | D3 (authored) | ECharts | Highcharts + module | ApexCharts | Plotly.js | Nivo | MUI X | uPlot |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| What the accessibility tree holds | nothing | legend list; an `application` element with no label, containing the tick text | image containing all the chart's text | nothing | named region, heading, generated description, a region per series | named `application`, legend buttons | two unnamed images, a toolbar | unnamed image | legend list, status region; the `title` label sits on a `role=\"none\"` element | title text, legend table |\n| Tab stops in the chart | 0 | 8, of which 7 land on unlabeled groups | 0 | 0 | 2 | 10 | 9: eight toolbar buttons and a link | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n| Arrow keys move between points | no | yes | no | no | yes | yes | no | no | yes | no |\n| Focused point's text reaches a live region | no | yes, `assertive` | no | no | no: tooltip only, the polite region stayed empty | yes, polite | no | no | no: tooltip only | no |\n| Focused point shown visually | – | not in the screenshot | – | – | yes | yes | – | – | yes | – |\n| Tooltip with the pointer between points | none | all series | all series (authored) | all series | one series | all series | one point | one point | all series | all series, in the legend |\n| Follows a container resize without code | yes | yes | scales like an image | **no** | yes | yes | **no** | yes | yes | **no** |\n| Bars animate on first render | yes | yes | no | yes | yes | yes | no | yes | yes | no |\n| …and stop under reduced motion | **no** | yes | – | **no** | **no** | yes | – | **no** | yes | – |\n\n![After Tab and four keys](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-kbd.png)\n\n### 4.2 Findings\n\n**R11. Half of the ten give a keyboard or assistive-technology user nothing to work with by default.** Chart.js and ECharts put one unlabeled canvas on the page. uPlot adds a title and a legend table that reads `--` until a mouse moves. Nivo is an unnamed image; the authored D3 chart is an image whose only content is its tick text. Chart.js' documentation leaves making the canvas accessible to the author (**Docs**, page dated 2021).\n\n**R12. Four configurations have keyboard navigation, with rough edges.** It was on by default in Recharts, ApexCharts and MUI X (the ApexCharts and MUI X documentation confirm the default), and in Highcharts once its module is imported. Observed rough edges:\n\n- Recharts and ApexCharts use `role=\"application\"`, which changes how screen readers pass keys through. Recharts' element has no label of its own. Leaving the Recharts chart took eight Tab presses.\n- ApexCharts has ten tab stops on this chart, six of them toolbar buttons for a zoomable time axis. **Inferred**: a dashboard of such charts multiplies that.\n- Highcharts and MUI X moved a visual focus marker and tooltip, but in this probe the point's text never reached a live region or the focused element's name. Whether a screen reader conveys it another way was not tested.\n- MUI X's documentation says the feature is still being developed (**Docs**).\n- Plotly's tab stops are its toolbar; the data cannot be reached.\n\n**R13. One configuration describes the chart.** Highcharts with its module generated: \"Line chart with 3 lines. The chart has 1 X axis displaying Time. Data ranges from 2026-01-01 00:00:00 to 2026-04-30 00:00:00. The chart has 1 Y axis displaying Sessions. Data ranges from 2732 to 48085.\" No tested configuration offers a data table without a further module.\n\n**R14. The bar entry animation ignores reduced motion in four of the seven that animate.** Chart.js, ECharts, Highcharts and Nivo produced the same early-frame difference with `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` as without. None of their packages contains that media query string ([`spot-checks.json`](../research/harness/spot-checks.json)). Recharts, ApexCharts and MUI X stop.\n\n**R15. Tooltips differ in basic behavior.** With the pointer in the plot between points, Chart.js shows nothing; its default requires the pointer to intersect a point. Highcharts, Plotly and Nivo describe one series or point; the others list all series at that x. MUI X's tooltip extended past the container's right edge.\n\n**R16. Three libraries do not follow their container.** ECharts, Plotly and uPlot stayed 720 px wide when the container shrank to 360 px. Each documents a resize call or option. uPlot needs pixel sizes up front.\n\n![Created at 340 px](../research/harness/shots/_sheet-narrow.png)\n\n### 4.3 Accessibility with each library's switch off and on\n\nThe first version compared Highcharts with its module against nine libraries without an equivalent. Both states, where a single documented switch exists:\n\n| Library | Switch | Off | On |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Highcharts | Import `modules/accessibility` | One image named by the title; 0 tab stops; a console message recommends the module | Named region, generated description, keyboard navigation, 2 tab stops |\n| ECharts | `aria: { enabled: true }` (no extra import with the full build) | One unlabeled canvas | `role=\"img\"` with a generated label that lists the first ten items of each series, timestamps as raw numbers; still 0 tab stops |\n| MUI X Charts | `title` prop | Legend list and status region; no named chart | The label is set on an element with `role=\"none\"`, so no named chart appeared in the tree |\n| Recharts, ApexCharts | On by default | – | As in 4.1 |\n| Chart.js, Plotly.js, Nivo, uPlot, D3 | None found | As in 4.1 | – |\n\nSo \"Highcharts is the strongest\" holds only with its module, which is one import. By default it is in the weaker half.\n\n## 5. How developers create, update and maintain\n\nCustomization was not compared (section 2).\n\n### 5.1 What each library needed beyond data\n\n**Observed** while writing the harness ([`research/harness/src/libs/`](../research/harness/src/libs)). These are what one author needed, not proof that no shorter way exists.\n\n| Library | Had to know or add |\n|---|---|\n| Chart.js | A date adapter package before a time axis works. `maintainAspectRatio: false` to fill a fixed-height container. |\n| Recharts | Reshape the data into row objects. A color for every series. A tick formatter for a numeric time axis. Eight components for one line chart. |\n| D3 | Everything: title, legend, readout and axes are authored, with no keyboard access. |\n| ECharts | `tooltip` and `legend` are absent unless declared. |\n| Highcharts | The accessibility module is a separate import. |\n| ApexCharts | A numeric height: `height: '100%'` produced a 456 px chart in a 400 px container. |\n| Plotly.js | Nothing extra. The shortest module, and 1,450 KiB gzipped with the documented import. |\n| Nivo | Margins by hand, or axes are clipped. `useMesh` for a tooltip. A five-property object for a legend. A canvas line component exists and was not tested. |\n| MUI X Charts | `@mui/material` and `@mui/system` as peer dependencies. It also accepts row objects through a `dataset` prop, which the harness did not use. |\n| uPlot | Pixel width and height, a stroke color per series, x in seconds (or an option to accept milliseconds), a path builder plus a custom axis for bars. |\n\n### 5.2 Findings\n\n**D1. Line counts are similar; hidden knowledge is not.** What separates the libraries is the list above: things that give a broken or blank chart with no message when missed.\n\n**D2. Five data shapes for the same table.** Parallel arrays with labels (Chart.js, uPlot), `[x, y]` pairs per series (ECharts, Highcharts, ApexCharts), row objects keyed by series (Recharts), `{ id, data: [{ x, y }] }` (Nivo), and separate x and y arrays (Plotly, MUI X). The harness fixture is columnar and had to be reshaped a different way for each.\n\n**D3. Seven update idioms.** Mutate then `chart.update()` (Chart.js); `setOption` (ECharts); `series.addPoint` (Highcharts); `updateSeries` (ApexCharts); `Plotly.extendTraces`; `setData` (uPlot); re-render with new props (the React three).\n\n**D4. Bundle cost spans a wide range** with the documented imports: 23 KiB to 1,450 KiB gzipped for the harness modules (section 1.4, with its caveats).\n\n**D5. Errors do not help.** The one exception in the study came from minified code and named neither the option nor the data.\n\n**D6. Release cadence ranges from none to very frequent.** Nivo and uPlot have had no release in 18 and 20 months. ApexCharts shipped four major versions in 16 months. Whether either is a problem for a user depends on what broke, which this study did not test.\n\n**D7. Licensing splits the field.** Highcharts is paid for commercial use; ApexCharts is free only below a size threshold; MUI X charges for zoom and pan. The fully permissive libraries include the ones with the weakest accessibility defaults, plus Recharts and MUI X's free tier.\n\n### 5.3 Load\n\nTwo metrics from headless Chrome 157 on one desktop machine, median of three runs.\n\n- **Main-thread task time to draw**: time the browser reports its main thread busy, from the create call until 1.6 s after the second animation frame, long enough for entry animations to finish. It counts work however the library schedules it. The first row is the same measurement with no chart.\n- **Main-thread task time per update** while streaming: the same counter over 300 updates, divided by 300.\n\n| Library and configuration | 10,000 points | 100,000 points | 1,000,000 points | Streaming: main-thread time per update | Frame callbacks later than 20 ms (of 300) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| No chart (measurement floor) | 8 ms | 8 ms | 9 ms | 0.1 ms | 0 |\n| Chart.js, defaults | 1.2 s | 2.3 s | 13.7 s | 10.7 ms | 2 |\n| Recharts, defaults | 2.5 s | 9.4 s | no result in 45 s | 15.2 ms | 17 |\n| D3 (authored: one SVG path, x axis redrawn) | 27 ms | 87 ms | 521 ms | 1.1 ms | 0 |\n| ECharts, defaults | 1.4 s | 2.8 s | 12.9 s | 15.7 ms | 30 |\n| Highcharts, defaults | 114 ms | 399 ms | 2.7 s | 3.5 ms | 0 |\n| ApexCharts, defaults | 83 ms | 134 ms | 636 ms | 9.7 ms | 3 |\n| Plotly.js, defaults | 51 ms | 119 ms | 365 ms | 6.1 ms | 0 |\n| Nivo, defaults | 733 ms | 5.0 s | no result in 45 s | 27.1 ms | 247 |\n| MUI X Charts, defaults | 99 ms | 366 ms | 3.0 s | 4.0 ms | 0 |\n| uPlot, defaults | 41 ms | 52 ms | 97 ms | 1.0 ms | 0 |\n| Chart.js, tuned configuration | 75 ms | 73 ms | 165 ms | 2.0 ms | 0 |\n| ECharts, tuned configuration | 65 ms | 119 ms | 512 ms | 2.2 ms | 0 |\n| Recharts, tuned configuration | 104 ms | 563 ms | 5.9 s | 4.7 ms | 0 |\n| Highcharts, Boost module (WebGL, software-rendered here) | 103 ms | 153 ms | 513 ms | 2.8 ms | 0 |\n| Plotly.js, `scattergl` (WebGL, software-rendered here) | 215 ms | 935 ms | 8.2 s | 17.3 ms | 23 |\n\nHow far these numbers can be trusted:\n\n- Three runs on one machine. Read them for order of magnitude.\n- Main-thread time does not include rasterization done on other threads, which matters for large SVG paths.\n- The streaming producer waits for an animation frame before each update, so a slow library receives a slower feed, and \"later than 20 ms\" counts late callbacks, not dropped frames.\n- The first version reported only the synchronous part of each update call. By that measure uPlot took under 0.1 ms; its redraw is deferred, and the real cost is about 1 ms.\n- The first version's D3 rows were invalid: the authored chart pinned its y axis at zero while the test data went negative, so most of the line was outside the plot. The rows above are from the corrected chart. Its y axis still does not follow the stream.\n- The tuned configurations are whole configurations. Chart.js' changes input format, parsing, decimation, animation, markers and stroke width; Recharts' removes the legend and grid. They show what a different configuration costs, not what any one option does.\n- The two WebGL rows ran on SwiftShader, a software rasterizer (`spot-checks.json`). They say nothing about WebGL on a GPU.\n- No adapter except D3's was checked for showing the right domain and window after the fact.\n\n**D8. Whole configurations differ by one to two orders of magnitude.** Chart.js: 13.7 s by default against 0.17 s tuned at a million points. ECharts: 12.9 s against 0.5 s. The cause is not isolated to one option.\n\n**D9. Plain JavaScript on Canvas 2D sets a high bar.** uPlot drew a million points in about 0.1 s of main-thread time and updated a 600-point window in about 1 ms. Any claim that another technology is needed has to beat numbers of that order.\n\n**D10. One DOM node per point is slow; SVG as such is not.** Recharts and Nivo, which create an element per point by default, took 5 to 9 seconds at 100,000 points. The authored D3 chart and Plotly's default trace, which draw one SVG path per series, took about 0.1 s. Nivo's canvas component was not tested.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat apps/docs/pages/{getting-started,line,bar,donut,data,options,updating,theming,accessibility,diagnostics}.md'","aggregated_output":"# Getting started\n\nAfter this page you can install Axylo, draw a first chart from rows with npm or a script tag, use it inside a framework, get the five things right that most often make a daily chart wrong, and know what to check when a chart looks wrong.\n\n## Install\n\nWith npm and a bundler:\n\n```sh\nnpm install axylo\n```\n\nWith a script tag, load one file from the package's `dist` folder: `axylo.iife.js` is the development build (it prints hints), `axylo.iife.min.js` the production build. Both define one global, `Axylo`.\n\nThe package has no runtime dependencies. Its module build comes in two flavours, chosen by the `development` export condition: a bundler that resolves that condition gets the development build, which also prints hints; otherwise you get the production build, which prints errors and warnings only. The styles need no import: the library adopts its stylesheet as a constructed style sheet, or, where that is not possible, adds a `<style>` element. A page that prefers to load the stylesheet itself can link `axylo/axylo.css`, and the library then uses that copy. The packed packages are tested in Chrome under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline`, with the stylesheet adopted and with it linked (`tests/dist/csp.browser.test.ts`).\n\nTypeScript 5.4 or later gives you checked field names. The package's declarations refer to DOM types (`HTMLElement`), so a TypeScript project needs the `DOM` library in `lib`, also for code that only calls `validate()` in Node. Plain JavaScript works the same without types.\n\n## The first chart\n\nEach of these runs live below its source. They are kept in the repository as the files the package's distribution tests install and render (`examples/first-chart`), and each says what page it needs around it.\n\nWith npm and a bundler:\n\n[example: first-chart-npm](../../../examples/first-chart/npm.ts)\n\nWith a script tag:\n\n[example: first-chart-script](../../../examples/first-chart/script.html)\n\nPoint at a chart, or press Tab to reach its plot and use the arrow keys, or open its table. The only required options are `data` and the fields: `x` and `y` (and `series` for long rows), or `label` and `value` for a donut. `title` is not required, but without it (or `labelledBy`) the chart gets a generated name and warning [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140).\n\n## Using Axylo in a framework\n\nNo framework adapter ships in 1.0; adapters for React, Vue, Svelte and others are planned after 1.0. Axylo is called from any framework's own hooks: create the chart when its element exists, call `update()` when your data or options change, and call `destroy()` when the element goes away. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element is supported; it is the sequence a framework's development mode produces.\n\nThe lifecycle as three plain functions:\n\n[example: lifecycle](../examples/lifecycle.ts)\n\n## Daily data: five things to get right\n\nThe first chart above has months as calendar dates and numbers as numbers. Data from an API is often less tidy. Here is a daily metric as many back ends send it, and the chart that reads it correctly:\n\n[example: daily-sessions](../examples/daily-sessions.ts)\n\n1. **Days sent as UTC midnights.** For daily totals sent as `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'` (or as epoch milliseconds of a UTC midnight), use `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. Without it they are instants, shown in the viewer's time zone, so west of UTC each value appears on the day before, with only hint [AX111](diagnostics.md#ax111). Strings such as `'2026-02-24'` need no `type`. See [the three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time).\n2. **Numbers sent as strings.** For decimal strings, use `y: { field: 'sessions', parse: 'number' }`. They are converted to JavaScript numbers, which are exact for integers up to 9,007,199,254,740,991; a larger count is rounded without a warning, so check exact counts with `Number.isSafeInteger` first. A field that mixes numbers and strings is not converted at all. See [numbers sent as strings](data.md#numbers-sent-as-strings).\n3. **Row order.** Pass rows in any order. Axylo sorts dated rows by x and keeps each value with its own row; categories keep the order in which they first appear.\n4. **A day with no data.** When you know a day is missing, send it, with `sessions: null`. Axylo can infer a missing day only between two rows of a series that has at least three rows at a regular step, and never before the first row or after the last; with two rows, or an irregular series, the line runs straight across the missing day. See [missing values](data.md#missing-values).\n5. **A misspelled field.** On error [AX101](diagnostics.md#ax101), compare the field you named with the keys the message lists, which come from your rows, and use the suggested spelling when one is offered. With no rows at all the keys cannot be checked, so the error appears once data arrives. With typed rows in TypeScript the typo does not compile.\n\n## What you get without asking\n\n| Feature | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| Name and summary | `title` is the visible heading and the chart's accessible name. In a card that already has a heading, `labelledBy` names the chart with it. The chart's description is a generated summary: chart type, series, the x range with its time zone, the value range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and anything left out. `description` adds to it. |\n| Table view | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table, 100 rows per page; above 100 rows a pager and a \"Jump to\" field appear. |\n| Keyboard | Up to two tab stops while the table is closed: the plot, where the arrow keys move through the points and its value text describes the point in focus, and the toolbar, when the chart has controls. A compact chart has none. See [the keyboard map](accessibility.md#the-keyboard-map). |\n| Reading values | Pointing in the plot shows what is there, by the chart type's rule (nearest x for line and area, the band for bars, the nearest point within 24 px for scatter, the slice for a donut). The tooltip sits against the marks it describes, not at the pointer, and lets the pointer through; rest on a point to move onto its tooltip, click to pin it. In a small plot the same text goes in a strip under the plot. Escape closes it. See [reading values with a pointer](accessibility.md#reading-values-with-a-pointer). |\n| Colour and more than colour | Eight default colours that work in light and dark pages. Lines and areas also carry marker shapes, repeated in the legend and tooltip; bars take fill patterns past the eighth series. |\n| Page theme | Font, text colour and light or dark come from the page. The chart repaints when the page switches scheme. |\n| Size | The chart fills its container's width. With no height set, the plot is 16:9 of the width, between 160 and 480 px tall. It follows the container as it resizes, with no call from you. |\n| States | No rows shows \"No data to show.\" inside the plot, never an empty frame or an invented axis. `state` sets loading, empty and error messages of your own. |\n| Diagnostics | Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported with a code, the field and rows where they apply, and the fix. When values were left out or changed, the reader is told too. |\n\nThe four states, each a chart with no rows:\n\n[example: states](../examples/states.ts)\n\n## When it looks wrong: three things to check\n\n**1. Read the diagnostics.** Open the browser console. Every problem Axylo found is printed once per code per page as `[axylo AX…] \"Chart title\": what is wrong, where, and how to fix it`. The same list is on the chart as `chart.diagnostics`, current as soon as the call returns. Each code has a page under [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md). Hints, which are advice rather than problems, print in the development build only.\n\n**2. Check which kind of x Axylo read.** A date off by one day almost always means instants where calendar dates were meant (item 1 above). If the tooltip heading names a time zone, Axylo read instants. If the x labels are your strings in input order, it read categories.\n\n**3. Check the values without drawing.** `validate()` runs the same checks as creating the chart, in Node or in a test, with the same options as the chart:\n\n```ts\nimport { validate } from 'axylo';\n\nconst problems = validate('line', { title: 'Monthly visitors', data: rows, x: 'month', y: 'visitors' });\nfor (const d of problems) console.log(d.code, d.level, d.message);\n```\n\nNumbers that arrive as strings, a missing day, two rows for the same day and a misspelled field each have their own code; [Your data](data.md) covers each case.\n\n## Next\n\nRead [Your data](data.md) next: it explains the three shapes rows can take, how dates are read, and what happens to missing values.\n# Line charts\n\nAfter this page you know when to use a line chart, which options it takes, and how a line is picked, read by keyboard, zoomed and simplified.\n\n## When to use it\n\nA line shows how a measure changed along an ordered x: sessions per day, latency per minute, revenue per month. Several series compare trends. The x can be calendar dates, instants, wall-clock times, numbers or categories.\n\nUse a [bar chart](bar.md) to compare a few categories, an [area chart](area.md) when the total and its parts matter, and a [compact line](compact.md) for a sparkline in a KPI tile.\n\n[example: line-time](../examples/line-time.ts)\n\n## Options\n\n```ts\nimport { line } from 'axylo';\nline(target, { data, x, y, series, ...options });\n```\n\n`target` is a CSS selector or an `HTMLElement`; a selector that matches nothing throws an error that names it.\n\n| Option | On a line chart |\n|---|---|\n| `x` | Dates, instants, wall-clock times, numbers or categories. See [the three kinds of time](data.md#three-kinds-of-time). |\n| `y` | One value field (with `series` for long rows, or alone for one series), or a list of fields for wide rows. |\n| `series` | For long rows only: the field that names each row's series. A single `y` needs no `series`. |\n| `zoom` | On by default for date, time and number x. `'touch'` also takes pinch and two-finger pan. |\n| `reference` | Lines and bands: `{ y: 500, label: 'Objective' }`, `{ x: [from, to], label: 'Incident' }`. |\n| `compact` | Marks only, for a tile. See [Compact](compact.md). |\n| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n\nNot on a line chart, and a type error if given: `stacked` (stacking lines hides how each part moves; a stacked area shows it), `layout` and `horizontal` (bars only), `label` and `value` (donut only). At run time an option that does not apply is ignored with [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150). The [options reference](options.md) has every option with its type and default.\n\n## How a line chart behaves\n\n| Behaviour | Line |\n|---|---|\n| Pointer | The nearest x is picked, wherever the pointer is in the plot. The tooltip, placed against the marks at that x, lists every visible series there (up to eight rows, then \"+N more\"). A series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\"; one whose value is missing reads \"No data\". A click pins the tooltip. In a plot smaller than 240 × 140 px the same text is written in a strip under the plot; see [reading values with a pointer](accessibility.md#reading-values-with-a-pointer). |\n| Keyboard | By x, then series. Left and Right move along x with every visible series at once; Up and Down narrow to one series and move between series; Escape returns to all series. |\n| Zoom | Date, time and number x: the Zoom in and Zoom out buttons (Pan left, Pan right and Reset zoom once zoomed), dragging across the plot or two clicks with Select range, the keys `+` `-` `0` and Shift with the arrows, the wheel while the plot has focus, and pinch with `zoom: 'touch'`. A category axis and a compact chart do not zoom. See [zoom](updating.md#zoom). |\n| Many points | When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified: in every device-pixel column, each run of valid samples keeps its minimum and maximum, and a break between runs is kept. Nothing is smoothed. Tooltip, keyboard and table read the data, not the drawing, so every sample stays reachable. |\n| Markers | Each series carries its marker shape at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip, so a dense line still has some. Every point gets a marker only when neighbouring points are at least 12 px apart. A point alone between gaps always has one. |\n| Gaps | A missing value you send (`null`) is a gap. A missing step with no row is a gap only when Axylo can infer it from the series (see [missing values](data.md#missing-values)). |\n| Many series | Eight series have eight colours. From the ninth, colours repeat and each series keeps its own combination of colour and dash or marker, with warning [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130). |\n| Order and duplicates | Drawn in x order whatever the input order. Two rows for the same x in one series are [AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108): that series is not drawn, the others are. |\n| Reference lines and bands | Drawn and labelled, included in the automatic value domain, and listed in the summary and the table caption. An author `domain` that leaves a reference out keeps it out of the drawing; the summary still lists it. |\n\nThe value axis of a line fits the data and need not start at zero, unlike a bar or area.\n\n## Many points\n\nA hundred thousand readings, drawn simplified and read exactly: press End on the plot to reach the last reading, or open the table.\n\n[example: line-dense](../examples/line-dense.ts)\n\n## More than eight series\n\n[example: line-many-series](../examples/line-many-series.ts)\n\n## Edge case: values that cannot be drawn\n\nNull is a gap without comment. `NaN`, a stray string and an absent field are gaps too, each with its own diagnostic, and the reader is told how many values are not shown. A value alone between gaps is a point.\n\n[example: line-dirty](../examples/line-dirty.ts)\n# Bar charts\n\nAfter this page you know when to use a bar chart, how to group, stack and turn bars horizontal, and what Axylo does with long labels, missing bars and negative values.\n\n## When to use it\n\nBars compare categories: satisfaction by team, tickets by product area, orders per region. A bar's length is its value, so the value axis always includes zero. Bars over a date axis compare counts per period (orders per day).\n\nUse a [line chart](line.md) to show a trend over many points, and a [donut](donut.md) only for the shares of one whole.\n\n[example: bar-target](../examples/bar-target.ts)\n\n## Options\n\n```ts\nimport { bar } from 'axylo';\nbar(target, { data, x, y, series, layout: 'grouped', horizontal: false, ...options });\n```\n\n| Option | On a bar chart |\n|---|---|\n| `x` | The categories (or dates, numbers). Categories keep the order of the data. `ordered: true` says they have a natural order (months, age bands), which allows thinning their labels. |\n| `y`, `series` | One value field (with `series` for long rows), or a list of fields. Several series make groups. |\n| `layout` | `'grouped'` (default) puts the series side by side; `'stacked'` stacks them. |\n| `horizontal` | `true` draws the bars from the left, with the categories down the side. Default: `false`. A `y` reference then runs up and down the plot, and an `x` reference across it. |\n| `zoom` | On date, time and number x only. A category axis does not zoom. |\n| `reference`, `compact` | As on a line chart. |\n| `domain` on `y` | Widened to include zero, with [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). |\n| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n\nNot on a bar chart: `stacked` (that is `layout: 'stacked'` here), `label` and `value` (donut only).\n\n## How a bar chart behaves\n\n| Behaviour | Bar |\n|---|---|\n| Pointer | The band under the pointer: the whole category, not only the bar. The tooltip lists every series in that category; a click pins it. See [reading values with a pointer](accessibility.md#reading-values-with-a-pointer). |\n| Keyboard | By category, then series. |\n| Zoom | Only on date, time and number x, as on a line. On a category axis `zoom()` changes nothing and is reported ([AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)). |\n| Baseline | The value axis always includes zero; an author `domain` without zero is widened ([AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110)). Grouped bars grow from zero. |\n| Missing values | A missing bar (a `null` or absent value, or a step Axylo infers is missing) gets a small \"no data\" mark, distinct from a zero bar: at the baseline for grouped bars, at the edge of the stack for stacked ones. A slot a series simply has no row for, as for a weekly series beside daily ones, gets no bar and no mark. |\n| Stacking | Positive parts stack up from zero, negative parts down from it, and the axis covers both. With mixed signs each bar shows two totals, of its positive and of its negative parts, and the readout names both (\"Total 165 k USD and -60 k USD\" in the example below); the bar does not show their difference. A missing part has zero height and the total is marked \"incomplete\"; a part is missing whenever its series has no value at that x, also when the series simply has no row there. |\n| Long labels | Remedies in order: thinning (date, number and `ordered` category axes only), wrapping to three lines, rotating up to 45°, truncating in the middle with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, the chart says \"Some labels hidden. See table.\" and [AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143) suggests `horizontal: true`. Axylo never switches orientation by itself: that would change what the chart says at a glance. |\n| Many bars | Not simplified. More x positions than pixels along the axis are drawn, with hint [AX132](diagnostics.md#ax132). |\n| Many series | Colour and fill pattern: from the ninth series bars get patterns, which gives 32 distinct combinations before they repeat; warning [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130) from the ninth. A [compact](compact.md) bar chart, which has no legend, gives neighbouring series different patterns from the second series on. |\n| Reference lines | Drawn and labelled, included in the automatic domain, listed in the summary and table caption. |\n\n## Horizontal and grouped bars\n\nLong category names read best down the side. With two value fields the bars are grouped, and a missing value is a \"no data\" mark, not an empty slot that looks like zero.\n\n[example: bar-horizontal](../examples/bar-horizontal.ts)\n\n## Edge case: stacks with both signs\n\n[example: bar-stacked](../examples/bar-stacked.ts)\n# Donut charts\n\nAfter this page you know when a donut is the right choice, which options it takes, and what it does with zero, missing, negative and too many slices.\n\n## When to use it\n\nA donut shows the shares of one whole: sessions by browser, budget by item. It works for a handful of parts; when more than eight have a value, Axylo groups the smallest. To compare amounts that are not parts of one whole, or many categories, use a [bar chart](bar.md).\n\n[example: donut-basic](../examples/donut-basic.ts)\n\n## Options\n\n```ts\nimport { donut } from 'axylo';\ndonut(target, { data, label, value, ...options });\n```\n\n| Option | On a donut |\n|---|---|\n| `label` | The field that names each slice. Labels must be unique. The label is also the slice's key, for `colors` and events. |\n| `value` | The value field: a field name or a [value field object](options.md#value-fields) (`label`, `unit`, `scale`, `format`). Values must not be negative. |\n| Common options | `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description`, `state`, `legend`, `tooltip`, `table`, `colors`, `messages`, `locale`, `strict`, `onDiagnostic`. |\n\nNot on a donut, and a type error if given: `x`, `y` and `series` (a donut has a label and a value), `zoom` and `reference` (there is no axis), `compact`, `stacked`, `layout`, `horizontal`. Without an axis there is nothing for `chart.zoom()` or a `tickFormat` on the value field to act on: both change nothing and are reported ([AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)).\n\n## How a donut behaves\n\n| Behaviour | Donut |\n|---|---|\n| Order | Slices keep the order of the data, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. |\n| Legend | Every slice, in slice order, with its value and its share as text. Shares have at most one fraction digit. Each entry has a button that selects its slice. |\n| Pointer | The slice under the pointer. The tooltip shows its label, value and share, in the hole when it fits there; a click pins it. The hole itself selects nothing. |\n| Keyboard | Through the slices in slice order. |\n| Zero and missing | A zero slice is listed in the legend without an arc. A slice with no value is listed as \"No data\". |\n| Negative | A negative value is error [AX120](diagnostics.md#ax120): the donut shows its error state, since a negative share cannot be drawn. |\n| More than eight | Counted among slices with a positive value only. When more than eight have one, the seven largest are drawn and the rest of those are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with [AX121](diagnostics.md#ax121). Zero and missing slices never count and are never grouped: each stays listed on its own. The table lists every category. \"Other\" has a key of its own and the muted colour. |\n| All zero | The empty state: there is no whole to divide. |\n| Duplicates | Two slices with the same label are [AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108): the donut is not drawn. |\n| Separators | Adjacent slices are separated by at least 1 px of background. |\n\n## Edge case: many slices, a zero and a gap\n\n[example: donut-many](../examples/donut-many.ts)\n# Your data\n\nAfter this page you can pass the same table as long rows, wide rows or typed columns; say which kind of time your x values are; control what happens to missing values; and give values a unit, a format and a percent scale.\n\n## One table, three shapes\n\nTake four days with two readings per day: sessions for two channels.\n\n| day | Direct | Referral |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-03-02 | 3,120 | 410 |\n| 2026-03-03 | 3,380 | 455 |\n| 2026-03-04 | 3,290 | 390 |\n| 2026-03-05 | 3,510 | 520 |\n\nAxylo accepts this table in three shapes. All three produce the same chart, the same table view and the same diagnostics; row numbers in diagnostics and events refer to your own data in the shape you gave it.\n\n### Long rows\n\nOne row per x and series, as most SQL queries and APIs return it. `series` names the field whose value says which series a row belongs to; that value is the series' key and its name.\n\n[example: data-long](../examples/data-long.ts)\n\n### Wide rows\n\nOne row per x, one field per series. `y` is a list of fields, and there is no `series`. Passing both is a type error, and at run time it raises [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150). Each field is a series: its key is the field name (`direct`), its name for the reader is the field's `label` (`'Direct'`). Use the key in `colors` and in event handlers, and the label for display. The series share one value axis: the first field's `domain`, `scale` and `tickFormat` set it (a `tickFormat` on a later field is reported, [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)), and with more than one field the axis is titled \"Value\" (the `value` message). The chart's generated name and its summary name the series rather than that axis (\"Line chart of Visits and Signups by day\", with fields labelled `'Visits'` and `'Signups'`). When the series have different units, the summary gives each series' range in its own unit and no range for the chart as a whole.\n\n[example: data-wide](../examples/data-wide.ts)\n\n### Typed columns\n\n`data: { columns }` with one array-like per field (an array or a typed array), all of the same length; different lengths are error [AX100](diagnostics.md#ax100). In a `Float64Array` or `Float32Array`, `NaN` means \"no value\" and raises nothing. This shape suits large data, because Axylo can read such a column in place; see [what Axylo reads, keeps and copies](data.md#what-axylo-reads-keeps-and-copies).\n\n[example: data-columns](../examples/data-columns.ts)\n\n## Fields\n\nA field is a key of your rows, written as a string, or an object when it needs more:\n\n```ts\nx: 'day'\ny: { field: 'sessions', label: 'Sessions', unit: 'visits' }\n```\n\nThe object form adds a label (the axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements), a unit, formats, a scale, a missing-value rule and author bounds. The [options reference](options.md#value-fields) lists them all.\n\nWith TypeScript, field names are checked against your row type. A misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, or wide `y` together with `series` does not compile. With untyped rows (from `fetch().json()`, say) the same mistakes are reported at run time: a field that no row has is [AX101](diagnostics.md#ax101), and the message lists the keys the rows do have and, when one is close, the closest match. With no rows, there is nothing to check a field against, and nothing is reported.\n\n## Three kinds of time\n\nDates are where charts most often go wrong without anyone noticing. Axylo keeps three kinds apart and never mixes them silently.\n\n| Kind | Looks like | Means | Shown |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Calendar date | `'2026-02-24'`, `'2026-02'`, `'2026'` | A day, month or year, wherever you are | The same in every time zone |\n| Instant | Epoch milliseconds with `type: 'time'`, a `Date`, `'2026-02-24T09:30:00Z'`, `'2026-02-24T09:30:00+01:00'` | One moment, the same everywhere | In `timeZone` (default: the viewer's zone), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the summary |\n| Wall-clock time | `'2026-02-24T09:30'` (no offset) | What a clock showed, zone unknown | As written, with hint [AX112](diagnostics.md#ax112) |\n\n### The off-by-one-day trap\n\nA daily metric is a calendar date. Many back ends send it as an instant at UTC midnight: `'2026-02-24T00:00:00Z'`, or the epoch milliseconds of that moment. For a viewer in New York that moment is the evening of 23 February, so every daily value appears one day early. In Berlin it is still the 24th. Showing the instant in the viewer's zone is correct for an instant; the data said \"this moment\", not \"this day\".\n\nThe example below has the same five values sent both ways. Your browser's time zone decides how the first chart reads: west of UTC, its first point is on the 23rd.\n\n[example: dates](../examples/dates.ts)\n\nAxylo notices at least two distinct instants that all fall at the same time of day, whole days apart, and raises hint [AX111](diagnostics.md#ax111). The fix is one of:\n\n- Send calendar dates: `'2026-02-24'`. `new Date(ms).toISOString().slice(0, 10)` turns a UTC midnight into one.\n- Keep the values and declare `x: { field: 'day', type: 'date' }`. Axylo then reads each instant (string, number or `Date`) as the calendar day it falls on in UTC, as the [daily example](getting-started.md#daily-data-five-things-to-get-right) does.\n- If they really are instants and everyone should see UTC, set `x: { field: 'day', type: 'time', timeZone: 'UTC' }`.\n\n### How Axylo decides\n\nWhen `type` is not given, each value of the x field votes for a kind:\n\n- `YYYY-MM-DD`, `YYYY-MM` or `YYYY` strings for a calendar date;\n- date-time strings with `Z` or an offset, and `Date` objects, for an instant;\n- date-time strings without an offset for a wall-clock time;\n- finite numbers for a number. A bare number is never taken for an instant: epoch milliseconds need `type: 'time'`, and numbers that look like timestamps raise hint [AX109](diagnostics.md#ax109);\n- anything else for a category.\n\nCategories win when they are at least as many as all the other votes together. Otherwise the kind with the most votes wins, ties going to date, then instant, then wall-clock time, then number. Values of any other kind are then unusable: they are not drawn, and they raise [AX107](diagnostics.md#ax107) with a note to the reader. Three category strings, two dates and two numbers therefore make a date axis that keeps the two dates. When a feed mixes kinds, declare `type`.\n\nCategories keep the order in which they first appear.\n\n`timeZone` takes `'local'` (the default, the viewer's zone), `'UTC'`, or an IANA zone such as `'Europe/Paris'`. It applies to instants. A zone the browser does not know is [AX152](diagnostics.md#ax152), and the viewer's zone is used.\n\nCalendar periods other than years and months (quarters, ISO weeks) are categories in 1.0: write them as you want them read (`'2025 Q1'`) and set `ordered: true` on the x field so that their labels may be thinned when they do not fit.\n\n## Missing values\n\nAxylo never draws a missing value as zero unless you ask, and does not connect a line across a missing value it knows about. It knows about one when your data says so:\n\n| In your data | Drawn | Reported |\n|---|---|---|\n| `null`, or `''` | A gap | Nothing: this is how to say \"no data\" |\n| `NaN` in a `Float64Array` or `Float32Array` | A gap | Nothing |\n| The value field absent from a row (`undefined`) | A gap | Warning [AX102](diagnostics.md#ax102), and the reader's note |\n| The x or series field absent from a row | The row is not drawn | Warning [AX102](diagnostics.md#ax102), and the reader's note |\n| `NaN` or `Infinity` in rows | A gap | Warning [AX103](diagnostics.md#ax103), and the reader's note |\n| `0` | Zero | Nothing |\n\nA day with **no row at all** is different: the data says nothing about it. Axylo infers that a step is missing only from a series' own rows, when the x axis is dates, instants, wall-clock times or numbers and the series is regular: it has at least three rows, every interval between neighbouring rows is a whole multiple of the smallest one, and at least half of the intervals are the smallest one. A missing step it infers is drawn as a gap, as if the row held `null`, with hint [AX105](diagnostics.md#ax105). It infers nothing before the first row or after the last, in a series of one or two rows, in an irregular series, or when there would be more than twice as many missing steps as rows (and more than 1,000). Another series having a row at that x does not make the step missing in this one: a slot a series simply has no row for (a weekly series beside a daily one) is not a gap, its line is joined across it, and a bar chart draws no bar and no \"no data\" mark there. In a stack it is different, see below. So **when you know a day is missing, send it with `null`**: that is the instruction Axylo always follows.\n\nA value standing alone between two gaps is drawn as a point, so it does not vanish. On a bar chart a missing value gets a small \"no data\" mark (at the baseline, or for a stacked bar at the edge of its stack), distinct from a zero bar. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". When a warning means values were left out, the chart shows a short note to the reader (\"Values not shown: 3. See table.\") inside the figure, in every build.\n\n**In a stack** (stacked areas or bars), a series with no value at an x is a missing part, whatever the reason: a `null`, an inferred missing step, or no row there at all. The part has zero height, the series' line follows its band, and the total at that x is marked \"incomplete\" in the tooltip, the table and announcements, so a stack never shows a total made of values the data does not contain. A weekly series stacked with a daily one is incomplete on the days between its rows; see [the area chart's example](area.md#edge-case-a-missing-part-of-a-stack).\n\nFor counts, where a day without a row means nothing happened, declare it: `missing: 'zero'` turns null, absent and inferred-missing values of that field into zeros, and the note says the values are shown differently from the data.\n\n[example: missing](../examples/missing.ts)\n\nHere are the values an application sends by mistake, all at once. The note under the chart is what the reader sees; the console has one line per code.\n\n[example: line-dirty](../examples/line-dirty.ts)\n\n## Numbers sent as strings\n\nSome database drivers return counts and decimals as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery). Axylo looks at the whole field:\n\n- When every value is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`, and exponent notation such as `'1e3'`), the field is converted and drawn, and hint [AX104](diagnostics.md#ax104) asks you to say so with `parse: 'number'`. With that, there is no hint, and TypeScript accepts the string field on a value axis.\n- When the field also holds a number, or any string that is not a plain decimal (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`), or another kind of value (`true`), every string in it is rejected, plain decimals included. They are not drawn, and they are warning [AX106](diagnostics.md#ax106).\n\nThe conversion is to JavaScript numbers. Integers are exact up to 9,007,199,254,740,991 (`Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`); a larger one is rounded to the nearest number JavaScript can hold, without a diagnostic: `'9007199254740993'` reads 9,007,199,254,740,992. A string too large for a number (`'1e999'`) becomes infinite and is [AX103](diagnostics.md#ax103). When exact counts could exceed that limit, check them with `Number.isSafeInteger(Number(value))` before charting.\n\n[example: numeric-strings](../examples/numeric-strings.ts)\n\n## Order and duplicates\n\nRows can come in any order. Line, area and scatter charts are drawn in x order, and each value stays with its own row; categories keep the order in which they first appear.\n\n[example: unsorted](../examples/unsorted.ts)\n\nOn a line, area or bar chart one x has one value per series. Two rows with the same x in the same series are error [AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108): that series is not drawn, while the other series are; with no series left to draw, the chart shows its error state and keeps its table. Axylo does not know whether you meant the sum, the last or the average. Two donut slices with the same label are the same error, and the donut is not drawn. On a scatter chart repeated x values, and repeated points, are ordinary data.\n\n## Units, formats and percentages\n\n| Option on a value field | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `label` | The axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements. Default: the field name. |\n| `unit` | Follows every value in tooltip, table and announcements, and is part of the axis title. |\n| `format` | How values are written in tooltip, table and announcements: `Intl.NumberFormat` options, or a function. Default: the locale's grouping, the full integer part and up to six significant fraction digits (`123,456.75`). |\n| `tickFormat` | How axis ticks are written. Default: the locale's grouping with the fraction digits the tick step needs (`50,000`); compact (`1.2M`) when the largest tick is at least a million; scientific from 10¹⁵. |\n| `scale: 'percent'` | The values are percentages from 0 to 100. |\n| `scale: 'ratio'` | The values are fractions from 0 to 1, shown as percentages. |\n\nA percent or ratio scale is declared, never guessed from a label. Its axis ends at full scale (100%) only when the largest value is at least half of full scale and not above it; otherwise it ends at the first tick at or above the data, so 4% is not drawn as a sliver of 100%. Numbers and dates are formatted for the `locale` option if given, else the document's language (`<html lang>`), else the browser's. A formatter that fails (a function that throws, or `Intl` options that `Intl` rejects) is warning [AX160](diagnostics.md#ax160), and the default format is used for that value.\n\n[example: units](../examples/units.ts)\n\nBar and area value axes always include zero, since a bar's length and an area's height are their values. A `domain` that leaves zero out is widened, with warning [AX110](diagnostics.md#ax110). Line and scatter axes fit the data.\n\nValue axes (y, and both axes of a scatter chart) end on a drawn tick at or beyond the data, the reference lines and bands, and stack totals; in a plot too narrow to label an end tick, the axis takes the next coarser step. A date, time or number x axis of a line, area or bar chart spans exactly its data instead (for bars, plus half a band at each end), so a time series is not padded to the next round tick.\n\n## What Axylo reads, keeps and copies\n\n- **Rows** are read when the chart is created or updated, and never changed. Their values are copied into Axylo's own typed arrays. Events hand back your own row object (`point.row`), and diagnostics name rows by their index in your data. A position no row of yours holds (an inferred missing day, a donut's \"Other\") has `row: null` and `index: -1`; check before you look a row up.\n- **Typed columns** of values (`Float64Array`, `Float32Array`) are read in place, without a copy, when Axylo can: the x values are already in order, no missing step is inferred, and no value has to change (no `Infinity`, no `missing: 'zero'` to apply). Otherwise Axylo copies what it needs; grouping long columns into series and scatter charts can need new storage too. Either way, do not change an array after passing it: to change the data, pass new arrays with `update({ data })`.\n- **Appended rows** are read once and added to the chart's own storage; `keep` limits how much is kept. See [Updating, streaming and zoom](updating.md#append-and-keep).\n\n## Checking data before you draw\n\n`validate(type, options)` runs the same reading and checking as a chart, without a page, and returns the diagnostics. Use it in Node, in a unit test, or in a coding agent's loop before rendering. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests).\n# Options reference\n\nAfter this page you can look up any option of any chart type, any field option, and every member of the chart handle, with its type, its default and what it does. The groups follow the declarations in `packages/axylo/src/public.ts`, which are the source of truth. Tests check that every key of those declarations is here with the type written here, and that each default written in `code` is what the chart does when the option is left out.\n\nTypes are written in TypeScript. `Row` is the type of one of your rows (for typed columns it is inferred from the columns), `Options` the options type of the chart, and `XValue` is `number | string | Date`, a value on an x axis.\n\n## Which options apply where\n\n| Group | line | area | bar | scatter | donut |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Common options](#common-options) | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes |\n| [Encoding](#encoding-line-area-bar) (`x`, `y`, `series`) | yes | yes | yes | – | – |\n| [Cartesian options](#cartesian-options) (`zoom`, `reference`) | yes | yes | yes | yes | – |\n| [`compact`](#compact) | yes | yes | yes | – | – |\n| [`stacked`](#area-options) | – | yes | – | – | – |\n| [`layout`, `horizontal`](#bar-options) | – | – | yes | – | – |\n| [Scatter encoding](#scatter-options) | – | – | – | yes | – |\n| [Donut encoding](#donut-options) (`label`, `value`) | – | – | – | – | yes |\n\nAn option that does not apply to a chart type does not compile, and at run time is ignored with [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150).\n\n## Common options\n\nOn every chart type.\n\n<!-- options: CommonOptions -->\n| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `data` | `Data<Row>` | required | The rows (`readonly Row[]`), or `{ columns }` with one array or typed array per field, all of equal length. See [Your data](data.md). |\n| `title` | `string \\| Title` | none | Visible heading and accessible name. Without it (and without `labelledBy`) the name is generated, with AX140. In a compact chart it is the accessible name only. |\n| `labelledBy` | `string` | none | Id of an existing heading that names the chart, for cards that already show a title. |\n| `subtitle` | `string` | none | A line under the title. |\n| `description` | `string` | none | Added to the generated summary. The time zone and any omitted values are always stated. |\n| `state` | `'ready' \\| 'loading' \\| { error: string } \\| { empty: string }` | `'ready'` | Shows a loading, error or empty message inside the plot instead of the data. |\n| `legend` | `'auto' \\| false \\| 'top' \\| 'right' \\| 'bottom'` | `'auto'` | Where the legend goes, or `false` for none. `'auto'` places it under the plot. A chart with one series has no legend entries. |\n| `tooltip` | `boolean` | `true` | The pointer readout: the tooltip, or in a small plot the strip under it. With `false` nothing is pinned, and a click still selects. Keyboard reading is not affected. |\n| `table` | `boolean` | `true` | The \"View as table\" control. |\n| `colors` | `Readonly<Record<string, string>>` | none | A CSS colour per series key: the `series` value for long rows, the field name for wide rows, the slice label on a donut. |\n| `messages` | `Partial<Messages>` | built-in English | Replacements for text the reader sees. See [Messages](#messages). |\n| `locale` | `string` | the document's language | Locale for numbers and dates: this option, else `<html lang>`, else the browser's. |\n| `strict` | `boolean` | `false` | Throw on any error or warning found while checking a create, `update` or `append` call, from that call. |\n| `onDiagnostic` | `(diagnostic: Diagnostic) => void` | none | Called once for each diagnostic the chart newly finds, in every build. |\n\n### Title\n\nWhen `title` is an object:\n\n<!-- options: Title -->\n| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `text` | `string` | required | The title. |\n| `visible` | `boolean` | `true` | `false` keeps it as the accessible name but hides it visually. |\n| `level` | `2 \\| 3 \\| 4 \\| 5 \\| 6` | `3` | The heading level, to fit the page's outline. |\n\n## Encoding (line, area, bar)\n\nLong rows name one `y` and a `series`; wide rows give `y` as a list and no `series`. The two forms are exclusive. One series needs only a single `y`.\n\n<!-- options: Encoding -->\n| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `x` | `XField<Row>` | required | The x field: a key, or an [x field object](#x-fields). |\n| `y` | `ValueField<Row> \\| readonly ValueField<Row>[]` | required | The value field, or one field per series. See [Value fields](#value-fields). The first field sets the shared value axis's domain and scale. |\n| `series` | `SeriesKey<Row>` | none | The field whose value names and keys each row's series (long rows only). |\n\n## Cartesian options\n\nOn line, area, bar and scatter charts.\n\n<!-- options: CartesianOptions -->\n| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `zoom` | `boolean \\| 'touch'` | `true` | Zoom and pan, on date, time and number x axes only: a category axis and a compact chart never zoom. `'touch'` also takes pinch and two-finger drag on touch screens. |\n| `reference` | `readonly Reference[]` | none | Reference lines and bands. See [Reference](#reference). Not on compact charts. |\n\n### Reference\n\n<!-- options: Reference -->\n| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `x` | `XValue \\| readonly [XValue, XValue]` | none | A line at an x value, or a band between two. |\n| `y` | `number \\| readonly [number, number]` | none | A line at a value, or a band between two. On a horizontal bar chart it runs up and down the plot. |\n| `label` | `string` | required | Shown beside the line or band, and listed in the summary and the table caption. |\n\nA reference widens the automatic value domain to include it. An author `domain` that leaves it out keeps it out of the drawing; the summary still lists it.\n\n## Compact\n\n<!-- options: CompactOption -->\n| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `compact` | `boolean` | `false` | Line, area and bar only. Marks only, as an image with a text alternative. See [Compact](compact.md). |\n\n## Area options\n\n<!-- options: AreaOptions -->\n| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `stacked` | `boolean` | `false` | Stack the series; the tooltip adds the total, or both totals when signs are mixed. |\n\n## Bar options\n\n<!-- options: BarOptions -->\n| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `layout` | `'grouped' \\| 'stacked'` | `'grouped'` | Several series side by side, or stacked. |\n| `horizontal` | `boolean` | `false` | Bars from the left, categories down the side. |\n\n## Scatter options\n\nScatter takes one `y` (never a list) and an x that is a number, an instant or a date.\n\n<!-- options: ScatterOptions -->\n| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `x` | `ScatterXField<Row>` | required | The x field: like an [x field](#x-fields), with `type` limited to `'number'`, `'time'` or `'date'`, and no `ordered`. |\n| `y` | `ValueField<Row>` | required | The value field. |\n| `series` | `SeriesKey<Row>` | none | The field that groups points into series. |\n\n## Donut options\n\n<!-- options: DonutOptions -->\n| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `label` | `SeriesKey<Row>` | required | The field that names, and keys, each slice. |\n| `value` | `ValueField<Row>` | required | The value of each slice. Not negative. |\n\n## Value fields\n\nA value field is a key of `Row` whose values are numbers (or `null`), or an object. A key whose values are strings is accepted only in the object form with `parse: 'number'`.\n\n<!-- options: ValueField -->\n| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `field` | `NumberKey<Row> \\| StringKey<Row>` | required | The key of the row: a number field, or a string field with `parse`. |\n| `parse` | `'number'` | none | The field holds numbers as strings; convert them to JavaScript numbers (exact for integers up to 9,007,199,254,740,991). |\n| `label` | `string` | the field name | Axis title, and the series name in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n| `unit` | `string` | none | Follows values in tooltip, table and announcements: `'ms'`, `'USD'`. |\n| `scale` | `'percent' \\| 'ratio'` | none | Percentage data: `'percent'` is 0–100, `'ratio'` is 0–1. Never inferred. |\n| `format` | `Intl.NumberFormatOptions \\| ((value: number) => string)` | the locale's grouping, the full integer part and up to six significant fraction digits | How values are written in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n| `tickFormat` | `Intl.NumberFormatOptions \\| ((value: number) => string)` | the locale's grouping, with the step's fraction digits; compact from a largest tick of one million, scientific from 10¹⁵ | How axis ticks are written. |\n| `missing` | `'gap' \\| 'zero'` | `'gap'` | What a missing value means. `'zero'` is for counts where absence means none. |\n| `domain` | `readonly [number, number]` | from the data | Author bounds. On bar and area, widened to include zero (AX110). In a wide chart only the first field's applies. |\n\n## X fields\n\nAn x field is a key of `Row` whose values are numbers, strings or `Date`s, or an object.\n\n<!-- options: XField -->\n| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `field` | `XKey<Row>` | required | The key of the row. |\n| `type` | `'number' \\| 'time' \\| 'date' \\| 'category'` | inferred | `'date'`: calendar dates; `'time'`: instants; numbers are never inferred to be instants. See [how Axylo decides](data.md#how-axylo-decides). |\n| `ordered` | `boolean` | `false` | Category axes: the categories have a natural order, so labels may be thinned when they do not fit. |\n| `timeZone` | `'local' \\| 'UTC' \\| (string & {})` | `'local'` | For instants: the zone they are shown in. `'local'` is the viewer's; otherwise `'UTC'` or an IANA zone name. |\n| `label` | `string` | the field name | Axis title of a number axis; a date, time or category axis is titled only when you give one. |\n| `unit` | `string` | none | Follows x values in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n| `format` | `Intl.DateTimeFormatOptions \\| Intl.NumberFormatOptions \\| ((value: XValue) => string)` | from the kind of x | How x values are written in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n| `tickFormat` | `Intl.DateTimeFormatOptions \\| Intl.NumberFormatOptions \\| ((value: XValue) => string)` | from the kind of x | How x ticks are written. |\n| `domain` | `readonly [XValue, XValue]` | from the data | Author bounds for x. |\n\nFor scatter charts the x field takes the same keys except `ordered`, and `type` without `'category'`:\n\n<!-- options: ScatterXField -->\n| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `field` | `XKey<Row>` | required | As above. |\n| `type` | `'number' \\| 'time' \\| 'date'` | inferred | No `'category'`: a scatter relates two measures. |\n| `timeZone` | `'local' \\| 'UTC' \\| (string & {})` | `'local'` | As above. |\n| `label` | `string` | the field name | As above. |\n| `unit` | `string` | none | As above. |\n| `format` | `Intl.DateTimeFormatOptions \\| Intl.NumberFormatOptions \\| ((value: XValue) => string)` | from the kind of x | As above. |\n| `tickFormat` | `Intl.DateTimeFormatOptions \\| Intl.NumberFormatOptions \\| ((value: XValue) => string)` | from the kind of x | As above. |\n| `domain` | `readonly [XValue, XValue]` | from the data | As above. |\n\n## The chart handle\n\nEvery chart function returns a `Chart<Row, Options>`.\n\n<!-- options: Chart -->\n| Member | Type | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `update` | `(patch: Partial<Options>) => void` | Change options: a present key replaces, `undefined` restores the default, an absent key stays. See [Updating](updating.md#update-change-options). |\n| `append` | `(rows: readonly Row[], options?: { keep?: number }) => void` | Add rows at the end. |\n| `zoom` | `(domain: readonly [XValue, XValue] \\| null) => void` | Set the visible x range; `null` shows everything. Where the chart does not zoom (a category axis, a donut, a compact chart), it changes nothing and reports [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150). |\n| `on` | `<E extends keyof ChartEvents<Row>>(event: E, listener: (payload: ChartEvents<Row>[E]) => void) => () => void` | Listen to `hover`, `select` or `zoom`; returns a function that removes the listener. |\n| `diagnostics` | `readonly Diagnostic[]` | Diagnostics for the current options and data, current as soon as a call returns. |\n| `destroy` | `() => void` | Remove the chart and everything it attached. Later calls do nothing. |\n\n### append options\n\n<!-- options: AppendOptions -->\n| Key | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `keep` | `number` | every row | The number of distinct x values to retain; older ones are dropped. |\n\n### Events\n\n<!-- options: ChartEvents -->\n| Event | Type | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `hover` | `Point<Row> \\| null` | The datum under the pointer or the keyboard's point; `null` when nothing is picked. |\n| `select` | `Point<Row>` | The reader clicked a point, or pressed Enter or Space on it. |\n| `zoom` | `readonly [XValue, XValue] \\| null` | A `zoom()` call or the reader changed the visible range; `null` for the whole range. Not sent when the view moves with appended data. |\n\n<!-- options: Point -->\n| Point key | Type | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `series` | `string` | The series key: the `series` value for long rows, the field name for wide rows, the slice label on a donut. |\n| `x` | `XValue` | In the form `zoom()` accepts: a `Date` for an instant, `'YYYY-MM-DD'` for a calendar date, an ISO date-time without offset for a wall-clock time, the category, otherwise the number. |\n| `y` | `number \\| null` | The value; `null` for no data. |\n| `row` | `Row \\| null` | Your row; with typed columns, an object built from the columns at that index; `null` for a position no row holds. |\n| `index` | `number` | The row's index in your data; `-1` when `row` is `null`. |\n\n### Diagnostic\n\n<!-- options: Diagnostic -->\n| Key | Type | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `code` | `` `AX${number}` `` | Stable code, with a page under [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md). |\n| `level` | `'error' \\| 'warning' \\| 'hint'` | See [levels](diagnostics.md#levels-and-who-is-told). |\n| `message` | `string` | What is wrong, where, and how to fix it. |\n| `chart` | `string` | The chart's title, else its element id. |\n| `field` | `string` | The field or option, when it is about one. |\n| `rows` | `readonly number[]` | The first offending rows in your data, at most ten, when it is about rows. |\n| `count` | `number` | How many values or rows in all, when it counts them. |\n\n## validate\n\n`validate(type, options)` takes `'line'`, `'area'`, `'bar'`, `'scatter'` or `'donut'` and that type's options, runs the checks a created chart runs from its call without a DOM, and returns `Diagnostic[]`. It cannot find what needs a page: contrast, sizes, label fitting, formatters, or whether a `labelledBy` id exists. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md#validate-in-node-and-in-tests).\n\n## Messages\n\nEvery message the chart shows the reader can be replaced through `messages`: the notes, states, controls, the table's labels, the generated name and summary. Diagnostics for developers keep the registry's English wording. Placeholders in braces are filled in by the chart; an override should keep the ones it needs. `{seeTable}` is filled with `seeTable` when the chart has a table and removed when it does not (`table: false`), so a translated note that keeps it reads right either way:\n\n[example: messages-translated](../examples/messages-translated.ts)\n\nEvery key, with its built-in English text, read from the library when this site is built, and where the chart shows it:\n\n[table: messages](../../../packages/axylo/src/messages/index.ts)\n\n## Options that do not exist\n\nThere is no `width`, `height`, `responsive`, `theme`, `palette` or `animation` option. Size comes from the container, colours from [CSS](theming.md), and motion from the reader's settings: the first render is not animated, and nothing moves under reduced motion. Each of these names, like any unknown key, is ignored with [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150), which suggests the closest known option.\n# Updating, streaming and zoom\n\nAfter this page you can change a chart's options and data, feed it a live stream, set and follow its zoom, listen to its events and remove it, and you know the rules that keep every one of these calls predictable.\n\nA chart function returns a handle with six members:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = line('#sessions', options);\n\nchart.update({ title: 'Sessions' });                                   // change options\nchart.append([{ day: '2026-05-01', sessions: 41210 }], { keep: 120 }); // add rows\nchart.zoom(['2026-03-01', '2026-03-31']);                               // set the visible x range\nconst off = chart.on('hover', point => console.log(point));            // listen; off() stops\nchart.diagnostics;                                                     // what is wrong, right now\nchart.destroy();                                                       // remove the chart\n```\n\nThere is no `resize()`: the chart follows its container by itself.\n\n## The rules every call follows\n\n1. **Calls apply in the order you make them.** `update({ data })` followed by `append(rows)` appends to the new data.\n2. **Checking is immediate.** Each call reads and checks its input before it returns. `chart.diagnostics` is up to date as soon as the call returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call.\n3. **Drawing waits for the next frame.** All the calls made before the next animation frame are drawn once, together.\n4. **Everything switches together.** Marks, labels, the tooltip, keyboard navigation and the accessibility text move to the new state in the same frame. No frame shows half of one state and half of another.\n\n## update: change options\n\n`update(patch)` takes any options of the chart's type. For each key:\n\n| In the patch | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| Present with a value | Replaces the old value whole. There is no deep merge: `y: { field: 'revenue' }` replaces the whole `y`, label and unit included. |\n| Present as `undefined` | Returns that option to its default. |\n| Absent | Stays as it is. |\n\nA new `data` replaces the data, including anything appended. Changing only how values look (`domain`, `format`, `tickFormat`, `title`, `colors`) does not make the chart read the rows again; changing a field, its `label` or its `unit` does.\n\n[example: update](../examples/update.html)\n\n## append and keep\n\n`append(rows, { keep })` adds rows at the end.\n\n- When the new rows are at or after the last x, they are read and added without reading the earlier rows again. Rows before the last x are still accepted, but the chart rebuilds its data from all the rows it holds, which is slower, and raises warning [AX151](diagnostics.md#ax151); a `strict` chart rejects such a call instead.\n- `keep` is the number of distinct x values to retain; older ones are dropped. Without it, every row is kept.\n- When the view is zoomed to the latest edge, it moves with new data, keeping its width. Zoomed elsewhere, it stays where it is.\n- Appended rows are checked like any others, and a `strict` chart throws on a bad one.\n- Arriving data is not announced. The plot's value text, which describes the point in keyboard focus, is not rewritten when an append only changes the point count or position number; it is rewritten when the focused point's own values change or the point leaves the window. Whether a screen reader then speaks depends on the reader; this has not been tried with one.\n\n[example: stream](../examples/stream.html)\n\n### Pause and resume\n\nOnce a chart has been appended to, its toolbar shows a Pause control. While paused, new rows are taken and checked but not shown, and the view does not move, so a reader can study a moment of a live chart. Their diagnostics come with the `append` call, as when running: rows that bring a ninth series raise [AX130](diagnostics.md#ax130) then, and a `strict` chart rejects that call. Resume shows the data as it is then: everything that arrived, within `keep`. Pause is in the chart's own toolbar for the reader; there is no method for it.\n\n## zoom\n\n`zoom([from, to])` sets the visible x range, given in the form of the x axis: date strings or `Date` objects for calendar dates, `Date` objects or epoch milliseconds for instants, numbers for a number axis. `zoom(null)` shows everything. A range that cannot be read is reported and ignored; `zoom` never throws.\n\nZoom applies to the date, time and number x axes of full line, area, bar and scatter charts (scatter along x only in 1.0). A category axis, a donut and a compact chart do not zoom in 1.0. There `zoom()` changes nothing and reports [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150) when it is called: the diagnostic is printed and passed to `onDiagnostic`, and is not kept in `chart.diagnostics`. A compact chart does not keep the range for later either, so turning `compact` off does not apply it. On a scatter chart the whole range is the x axis as drawn, which ends on ticks (spec F7): in a narrow plot that axis can take a coarser step and a range wider than the data, and Zoom out and Reset zoom return to that range.\n\nWhere zoom applies, the reader has four ways to zoom:\n\n1. **Buttons, with single clicks.** Zoom in and Zoom out in the toolbar; once zoomed, also Pan left and Pan right (half a view each way, unavailable at an end) and Reset zoom.\n2. **Choosing a range.** Drag across the plot. Or, without dragging, press Select range (the symbol after Zoom in) and click or tap the two ends of the range, in either order; the first end is marked in the plot. While Select range is pressed, a click sets an end and neither pins a tooltip nor selects. The toggle releases itself after the second end; pressing it again or Escape releases it sooner, and a drag zooms as a drag and releases it too.\n3. **Keys,** while the plot has focus: `+` and `-` zoom, `0` shows everything, and Shift with Left or Right pans. The wheel zooms as well while the plot has focus, never with Ctrl or Meta held, so browser zoom and page scrolling keep working.\n4. **Pinch,** when `zoom: 'touch'` is set: two fingers zoom, and moving them pans.\n\nA one-finger drag always scrolls the page. `zoom: false` removes the reader's ways to zoom.\n\n## on: events\n\n`on(event, listener)` returns a function that removes the listener.\n\n| Event | Payload | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| `hover` | A point, or `null` when nothing is picked | The datum under the pointer, or the keyboard's point, changes. |\n| `select` | A point | The reader clicks a point or presses Enter or Space on it. |\n| `zoom` | `[from, to]`, or `null` for the whole range | A `zoom()` call, or the reader's zoom or pan, changes the visible range. Not when the view moves with appended data. |\n\nA point is `{ series, x, y, row, index }`:\n\n- `series` is the series key: the `series` field's value for long rows, the field name for wide rows (not its `label`), the slice label on a donut;\n- `x` is in the form `zoom()` accepts for the axis: a `Date` for an instant, `'YYYY-MM-DD'` for a calendar date, an ISO date-time without offset for a wall-clock time, the category's label, otherwise the number. The two ends of a `zoom` event take the same form, except that an end between two calendar dates stays a number, which `zoom()` reads as the same position. Either can be passed to another chart's `zoom()` unchanged. Do not turn a wall-clock time into a `Date`: that would read it in the viewer's zone;\n- `y` is the value, `null` for no data;\n- `row` is your own row object (with typed columns, an object built from the columns at that index), and `index` its index in your data. A position no row of yours holds (an inferred missing step, a donut's \"Other\") has `row: null` and `index: -1`.\n\n[example: zoom](../examples/zoom.html)\n\nA zoom event's range drives a second chart:\n\n[example: zoom-linked](../examples/zoom-linked.html)\n\n## diagnostics\n\n`chart.diagnostics` lists the diagnostics for the current options and data. It is current as soon as a create, `update` or `append` returns. Diagnostics found later, while drawing (contrast, labels that stop fitting after a resize, a formatter that fails), are added when found. See [Diagnostics](diagnostics.md).\n\n## destroy\n\n`destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects every observer and listener, removes what the chart added to its element, and makes later calls do nothing. Call it when the chart's element leaves your page; a new chart can then be created on the same element (see [using Axylo in a framework](getting-started.md#using-axylo-in-a-framework)). A chart whose element leaves the page without `destroy()` lets go of the page's listeners until the element returns.\n\n## Strict mode\n\nWith `strict: true`, any error or warning found while checking a create, `update` or `append` call throws from that call, and the chart keeps its last valid state. The thrown error is named `AxyloStrictError`, its message starts with the first diagnostic, and its `diagnostics` property has all of them. A rejected create leaves nothing behind in the page.\n\nHints never throw. Neither do diagnostics found later while drawing, nor an internal failure while drawing ([AX199](diagnostics.md#ax199)): those are reported when found. A failure while the call itself is checked (a row getter that throws) rejects the call.\n\n[example: strict](../examples/strict.html)\n\nStrict mode suits tests and development builds, where a bad row should stop you. In production a chart that shows a gap and tells the reader is usually better than one that throws.\n# Theming\n\nAfter this page you can change a chart's colours and type with CSS custom properties, give a series the same colour on every chart, and know what Axylo does in dark mode, in forced-colours mode and in print, and why its default palette looks the way it does.\n\nThere is no theme object and no palette option. A chart takes its font and text colour from the page, as any element does, and everything else from CSS custom properties that you can set on the chart, on any ancestor, or on `:root`.\n\n## The custom properties\n\nEach colour property falls back to a default from Axylo's stylesheet, with one value for light charts and one for dark. The defaults below are read from the packed package's stylesheet when this site is built.\n\n[table: theme tokens](../data/tokens.mjs)\n\nValues are any CSS colour, including `var()` references; a chart follows a variable when it changes, without a call. Colours of marks should keep at least 3:1 against the background behind the chart, and text 4.5:1. When the series colours fall below 3:1 against the background the chart actually sits on, Axylo raises [AX141](diagnostics.md#ax141); when that background cannot be resolved (a background image on the way up), it does not judge.\n\n[example: theme-tokens](../examples/theme-tokens.html)\n\n## One colour per series\n\nThe palette assigns colours by position: the first series gets the first colour. On a dashboard where charts show different subsets, \"Referral\" would change colour from chart to chart. `colors` pins a colour to a series key, so the same series looks the same everywhere:\n\n```ts\nconst colors = { Direct: 'var(--brand-direct)', Referral: 'var(--brand-referral)' };\nline('#a', { ...options, colors });\n```\n\nThe key is the series key: for long rows the `series` field's value (as above), for wide rows the field name, not its `label`, and for a donut the slice label. Series not named in `colors` take palette colours. A value the browser does not accept as a colour is replaced by the series' palette colour. A donut's generated \"Other\" slice always uses the muted colour.\n\n[example: theme-colors](../examples/theme-colors.html)\n\nWith wide rows, key the colours by field name:\n\n[example: wide-keys](../examples/wide-keys.html)\n\n## Dark mode\n\nEach chart decides on its own whether it is light or dark, from the page around it:\n\n1. If `--axylo-background` is set, from that colour's lightness.\n2. Otherwise from the chart element's computed `color-scheme`: `light` or `dark`; for `light dark`, the scheme the browser is using there.\n3. Otherwise from the inherited text colour: light text means a dark page.\n\nSo a page that sets `color-scheme`, a page whose theme switch only changes classes and colours, and a light-only page on a device set to dark mode (which stays light) all get the right defaults. The chart looks again when `prefers-color-scheme`, `forced-colors` or `prefers-contrast` change, and when the `class`, `style` or `data-theme` attribute of the chart or any of its ancestors changes, and repaints in the next frame. A theme switch that changes none of these (a stylesheet swapped in, say) is not observed.\n\n[example: theme-toggle](../examples/theme-toggle.html)\n\n## Forced colours, high contrast and print\n\nIn forced-colours mode (Windows High Contrast and similar) every paint becomes a system colour, the colours you set included, and they come back when the mode ends. Series are then told apart by their markers, dashes and patterns. Under `prefers-contrast: more`, and in forced colours, lines also differ by dash. No example on this site emulates forced colours; the library's own browser tests do.\n\nMarks are drawn as SVG paths, the provisional default of [decision 0002](../../../docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md), which keeps them sharp at any zoom. There is no print stylesheet in 1.0, and printing has not been tested.\n\n## The default palette\n\n[table: palette](../../../packages/axylo/src/theme/palette.ts)\n\nEight colours, in the order blue, orange, green, purple, red, cyan, magenta, slate, with the same family in the same slot in light and dark charts. They were fitted to measurable limits, not chosen by eye, and each set is measured against its own scheme's default background:\n\n- each colour is at least 3:1 against that background;\n- every pair stays at least 0.10 apart in OKLab under normal vision and under simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia, also after the simulated colours are rounded to 8-bit values; the first four, which most charts use, at least 0.14;\n- each stays at least 0.10 from the focus, reference and muted colours;\n- neighbouring colours stay at least 0.06 apart as 25% and 80% fills, as computed and as Chrome painted them.\n\nThe dark scheme's focus colour is a near-white, so that the first dark series could be an ordinary dark-mode blue. Colour is never the only cue: lines and areas also carry marker shapes, bars are told apart by position and named by the legend and tooltip (with fill patterns past the eighth series, and in a compact chart), adjacent fills are separated by background, and the legend and tooltip repeat the cues.\n\nThe owner adopted this palette on 2026-11-18, out of the candidates [decision 0003](../../../docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md) measured, for a more even light scheme, a calmer first three colours and a neutral eighth. The record states what it costs: a louder light red and purple, a dimmer dark purple, tighter dark margins to the focus and muted colours, and a deeper dark first blue. The palette it replaced is kept there under \"Superseded\", with its measurements. An independent recomputation found all sixteen colours within the limits above ([review](../../../reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/palette-b.review.md)).\n\n## Internal properties\n\nThe stylesheet also uses a few custom properties that are not for authors. They are listed so that you can recognise them in the developer tools; setting them is unsupported.\n\n[table: internal tokens](../data/tokens.mjs)\n# Accessibility\n\nAfter this page you know what an Axylo chart exposes to assistive technology, to the keyboard and to readers with low vision, high-contrast settings or a colour-vision deficiency; which keys do what; how values are read with a pointer; what you as the author must supply; and what has and has not been verified.\n\nThese features are on by default: there is no module to import and no option to turn on.\n\n## What the chart provides\n\n| For | What the chart provides |\n|---|---|\n| Assistive technology | The figure is a group with an accessible name (`title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or a generated name) and a description: the generated summary of type, series, x range with its time zone, value range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and anything left out. A compact chart is an image with the same name and description. The plot is one native range input whose value text describes the point in focus: its x, the value of each visible series with its unit, and its position (\"Point 4 of 120\"). Changes of state (loading, empty, error) are written once to a polite live region. \"View as table\" opens the data as an HTML table with a caption, column headers with units, and row headers. |\n| Keyboard | Up to two tab stops while the table is closed: the plot, and the toolbar when the chart has controls. A compact chart has none. Every point is reachable in order, the series can be switched, the ends are one key away, and Tab leaves the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px wide, in the focus colour, which meets 3:1 on the default backgrounds; it always shows while you use the keyboard in the plot, and it may also show after a click. |\n| Low vision | Text is HTML at 12 px or more by default, with 4.5:1 contrast for text and 3:1 for marks, axes and focus indicators in the default colours. Text follows browser zoom and the page's text-spacing settings. Labels never overlap; when one cannot be shown, the chart says so. The tooltip is placed against the marks it describes, not at the pointer, and does not cover them, a control or the focus ring; in a small plot its content is written in a strip under the plot instead (see [Reading values with a pointer](#reading-values-with-a-pointer)). |\n| Colour-vision deficiency | Eight colours chosen to stay apart under simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia, and never colour alone: lines and areas carry marker shapes, repeated in the legend and tooltip; bars by position and order, named in the legend and tooltip, and also by fill pattern past the eighth series and between neighbours in a compact chart, which has no legend; adjacent fills are separated by background. |\n| High contrast | In forced-colours mode the chart uses system colours, and lines also differ by dash; so they do under `prefers-contrast: more`. |\n| Reduced motion | Nothing is animated on first render. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing transitions at all. |\n| Touch and a single pointer | Every control is at least 24 × 24 px. Everything a drag does can be done with single clicks or taps: Zoom in and out, Pan left and right, and Select range for choosing a range with two clicks. A one-finger drag scrolls the page. |\n\nThe legend entries of a line, area, bar or scatter chart are toggle buttons with a pressed state; a hidden series keeps its full-contrast text, struck through. A chart with one series has no legend entries. A donut's legend lists every slice, each with a button that selects that slice (the chart fires `select`), without a pressed state.\n\n## The keyboard map\n\nThe plot is a single native range input laid over the chart. Its keys:\n\n| Key | In the plot |\n|---|---|\n| Left, Right | Previous or next x, with every visible series there. Scatter: the previous or next point in x, then y order. Donut: the previous or next slice. |\n| Home, End | The first or last point. |\n| Page Up, Page Down | Forward or back by one tenth of the number of points, rounded down, and at least one. |\n| Up, Down | Narrow to one series and move to the previous or next series. Donut: the next or previous slice. |\n| Escape | One thing per press, in this order: end a drag; release Select range; close the tooltip or end a pin: the datum on show stays closed until the pointer has reached a different datum, and nothing else opens at once (after Escape from a held tooltip, the datum under the pointer stays closed too); return from one series to all. When none of these applies, the key is left to the page, so a dialog around the chart closes only on an Escape the chart did not use. |\n| Enter, Space | Select the point: the chart fires its `select` event. |\n| `+`, `-`, `0` | Zoom in, zoom out, show everything (date, time and number x). |\n| Shift with Left or Right | Pan the zoomed view. |\n| Wheel | Zoom, only while the plot has focus and never with Ctrl or Meta held. |\n\nThe toolbar holds the controls that apply to the chart: legend toggles, Zoom in, Select range, Zoom out, Pan left, Pan right, Reset zoom, Pause, View as table. It is one tab stop. Inside it the arrow keys move between controls, Home and End go to the first and last, and Enter or Space presses the control. The open table adds the table's scroll region to the tab order, and, when there are more than 100 rows, its pager (previous page, next page and a \"Jump to\" field). The plot's accessible description gives a short version of this map.\n\nWith several charts on a page, an Escape belongs to the chart that has focus or, when focus is in none of them, to the one that showed something last; one press is used by one chart at most.\n\nThis pattern was chosen by reasoning and by inspecting the browsers' accessibility trees, not by testing with screen readers; see [decision 0001](../../../docs/decisions/0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md) and [decision 0004](../../../docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md).\n\n## Reading values with a pointer\n\nPointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the chart type's rule: on line and area charts the nearest x, on bars the band under the pointer, on scatter charts the nearest point within 24 px, on a donut the slice under the pointer. What is shown depends on the room the plot has:\n\n| Plot | What pointing shows |\n|---|---|\n| At least 240 × 140 px | A floating tooltip. |\n| Narrower than 240 px or shorter than 140 px | The same content, written as running text (\"x: series value; series value; +2 more\") in a strip of two lines reserved under the plot. The strip covers nothing, and nothing covers it. |\n| So small that the strip would leave the plot under 80 px high | No readout. The datum is highlighted; its values are in the plot's keyboard reading and in the table. |\n\nThe choice is made by layout, when the chart is laid out, never by the pointer.\n\n**Where the tooltip goes.** It is placed against the marks of the datum it describes, never at the pointer: above them first, then below, then to the right or the left (for horizontal bars: right, left, above, below); a donut's tooltip goes in its hole first, when it fits there. It stays inside the plot, the viewport and any container that clips the chart, and covers neither those marks, a control nor the focus ring; rows that do not fit are counted in \"+N more\". Only when not even one row fits anywhere may it partly cover the marks, never a control or the focus ring, and the table still has every value.\n\n**Long text.** A value that fits stays on one line with its unit; a longer one wraps inside the tooltip, which is never wider or taller than the visible part of the plot. However long the text, the tooltip shows its heading and at least one row, cut if they must be; it is left out only when every place for it would cover a control or the focus ring. In the strip, entries wrap within its two lines, and when not even one fits, the x and one row are cut to one line with an ellipsis. Its \"+N more\" is shown whole or the strip is not shown.\n\n**It never takes the pointer.** The pointer passes through a tooltip to the plot, so the readout follows every move and a click lands on the plot. To reach the tooltip, rest the pointer on the datum for a quarter of a second: the datum is then held while the pointer travels towards its tooltip and while it is over it. Leaving that path picks again at once; a pointer that moves without resting is never held. A click always acts on what is under the pointer, whatever is held.\n\n**A click or tap pins.** It selects the datum under the pointer (the `select` event) and pins its tooltip, or its strip. Pinned, the content takes the pointer and is exposed to assistive technology: each row is a button of at least 24 × 24 px that selects that series' value, and \"+N more\" becomes a button that shows the next rows. The rows are outside the tab order; from the keyboard, the plot's Up and Down reach the same series. The pin ends with Escape, a click elsewhere, a drag or pinch, or when its datum leaves the view. With `tooltip: false`, or in a plot with no readout, nothing is pinned; a click still selects. While Select range is pressed, a click sets an end of the zoom range instead, and neither pins nor selects ([zoom](updating.md#zoom)).\n\n## What you must supply\n\nAxylo can describe the data; only you know what it means.\n\n- **A meaningful name.** Give every chart a `title`, or, when the card around it already has a visible heading, `labelledBy` with that heading's id. A generated name (\"Line chart of sessions by day\") is a fallback and raises [AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140). A title can be hidden visually with `title: { text, visible: false }`, and its heading level set with `level` (2 to 6, default 3) to fit your page's outline.\n- **Series names.** For wide rows, give each field a `label`; for long rows, the series field's values are the names. They are part of every point's value text.\n- **Units.** Set `unit` (and `label`) on value fields, so that \"412\" is \"412 ms\".\n- **Context.** `description` adds what the data cannot say (\"The spike on 12 March was the payment provider outage.\") to the generated summary. It never removes the time zone or the statement of values left out.\n- **The page's structure.** Headings, landmarks and the order of content around the chart are yours.\n- **A pause for streaming compact tiles.** A full chart fed by `append` has its own Pause control. A compact chart has no toolbar, so it has none: if your tiles stream, give the reader a control of your own that stops appending, and append what arrived meanwhile on resume (WCAG 2.2.2).\n- **Text in other languages.** Every message the chart shows the reader can be replaced through `messages`; see the [translated example](options.md#messages). Diagnostics for developers keep their English wording. When the page's language is not English, the built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"`.\n\n[example: a11y-card](../examples/a11y-card.html)\n\n## What has and has not been verified\n\nAutomated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast and text. They do not establish that a person using a screen reader understands a chart.\n\n- **Automated checks** are the library's acceptance tests, run in Chrome on Linux and in Playwright's builds of Firefox and WebKit. WebKit results are Playwright's WebKit, not Safari. What the acceptance checker recorded for the revision of this site is on the [conformance page](conformance.md).\n- **No screen-reader rehearsal was done for 1.0.** No screen reader has been run against these charts, so nothing in this documentation says what a screen reader speaks. The plot's pattern ([decision 0001](../../../docs/decisions/0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md)) and the rules for the tooltip and the focused point's text ([decision 0004](../../../docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md)) are untried with a screen reader and with a screen magnifier.\n- **No usability testing with disabled people.** Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, and no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made.\n- **Not run at all:** Safari itself (macOS and iOS), Edge, Windows, macOS, and phones.\n- **Limits of 1.0:** right-to-left text renders, but the layout is not mirrored. A chart inside a rotated, skewed or mirrored element draws, but pointing and tooltip placement are wrong there; inside a translated or scaled element (any positive scale) they are exact. Built-in text ships in English only.\n\n## Conformance\n\nThe [conformance table](conformance.md) lists the WCAG 2.2 success criteria the specification cites, what Axylo does for each, and what the acceptance checker recorded for them when this site was built.\n# Diagnostics\n\nAfter this page you can read an Axylo diagnostic, collect diagnostics in your own code, make a chart throw on them, and check a chart's data in Node or in a test before anything is drawn. Every code has its own page, listed at the end.\n\n## Reading one\n\nWhen Axylo cannot draw something as given, it prints one line to the console:\n\n[output: AX103](../data/diagnostics.mjs)\n\n| Part | Here |\n|---|---|\n| Code | `AX103`. Stable across releases; each has a page. |\n| Chart | `\"Conversion\"`: the chart's title, or its element id when it has none. |\n| What and where | The field, how many values, and the first rows (up to ten), numbered in your own data as you passed it. A diagnostic about the chart as a whole (no name, too many series, low contrast) has no field and no rows. |\n| Fix | What to change. |\n| Outcome | What the chart did instead: here, gaps. |\n\n## Levels and who is told\n\n| Level | Means | Console |\n|---|---|---|\n| Error | Something cannot be drawn as given: the chart, a series, or the donut. | Every build, once per code per page, with `console.warn` |\n| Warning | Something is not drawn as given, or an option is ignored. | Every build, once per code per page, with `console.warn` |\n| Hint | Advice for you. | Development builds only, with `console.info` |\n\nThe level says who is told; it does not by itself say what stays on screen. That depends on the code, and each code's page says it, with a checked minimal input. For example: an error about one series ([AX108](diagnostics.md#ax108)) leaves the other series drawn; a warning can put the chart in its error state when the ignored option was needed to draw at all (a `y` list on a scatter chart, [AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)); and some hints come with a visible change: an inferred missing step is drawn as a gap ([AX105](diagnostics.md#ax105)), a plot too small to draw is replaced by its summary ([AX142](diagnostics.md#ax142)), hidden labels get a note ([AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143)).\n\nWhen values were left out or changed, the chart shows the reader a note in the figure (\"Values not shown: 3. See table.\", or \"Values shown differently from the data\"), in every build, and in the accessibility tree.\n\n## Collecting them in your code\n\nEach diagnostic is an object:\n\n```ts\ninterface Diagnostic {\n  code: `AX${number}`;           // 'AX103'\n  level: 'error' | 'warning' | 'hint';\n  message: string;               // what is wrong, where, and how to fix it\n  chart: string;                 // the chart's title, else its element id\n  field?: string;                // when it is about a field\n  rows?: readonly number[];      // first rows, in your data, at most 10\n  count?: number;                // how many values or rows in all\n}\n```\n\n`chart.diagnostics` lists them for the current options and data, up to date as soon as a create, `update` or `append` returns. `onDiagnostic` is called once for each diagnostic the chart newly finds, in every build and at every level, so it is the place to send them to your error tracking. A listener that throws is reported once and otherwise ignored; it cannot break the chart.\n\n[example: diagnostics-listener](../examples/diagnostics-listener.html)\n\n## When they are found\n\nMost diagnostics are found while a call checks its input: the create, `update` or `append` call. Those are in `chart.diagnostics` when the call returns. A few can only be found later, while drawing: low contrast after the page's CSS changes ([AX141](diagnostics.md#ax141)), a plot too small ([AX142](diagnostics.md#ax142)), labels that stop fitting after a resize ([AX143](diagnostics.md#ax143)), more bars than pixels ([AX132](diagnostics.md#ax132)), a formatter that fails ([AX160](diagnostics.md#ax160)), a `labelledBy` that names no element when there is no `title` ([AX140](diagnostics.md#ax140)), and an internal failure ([AX199](diagnostics.md#ax199)). They are reported when found, through the same console line and `onDiagnostic`. A `zoom()` call that cannot apply (a category axis, a donut, a compact chart) is reported when it is made, through the console line and `onDiagnostic` only: it changes nothing, so `chart.diagnostics` does not list it ([AX150](diagnostics.md#ax150)).\n\n## Strict mode\n\n`strict: true` makes the chart throw on any error or warning found while checking a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. The chart keeps its last valid state; a rejected create leaves nothing in the page. The error is named `AxyloStrictError`, and its `diagnostics` property has every diagnostic of the rejected call. Hints never throw, and neither do diagnostics found later while drawing. A failure while a call is being checked (such as a row getter that throws, [AX199](diagnostics.md#ax199)) rejects that call too, with AX199 after what it had found; a failure later, while drawing, is never thrown. Each code's page says whether a strict chart throws it. See the example in [Updating](updating.md#strict-mode).\n\n## validate() in Node and in tests\n\n`validate(type, options)` runs the same reading and checking as creating a chart (options, data, view, name) without a DOM, and returns the diagnostics a created chart would report from its call. It takes the same options as the chart type, so it type-checks the same way. Importing `axylo` in Node does not touch the DOM.\n\nUse it on a server, in a build step, or in a coding agent's loop:\n\n[source: examples/node/validate.ts](../examples/node/validate.ts)\n\nOr in a unit test, so that a change in the data a query returns fails the build instead of a dashboard:\n\n[source: examples/node/chart-data.test.ts](../examples/node/chart-data.test.ts)\n\nBoth files are type-checked and run by this documentation's own tests, against the packed package. The package's declarations refer to DOM types, so a TypeScript configuration for Node code needs `\"lib\": [\"ES2022\", \"DOM\"]` (or `skipLibCheck`). `validate()` cannot find what only a page can show: the diagnostics listed under \"When they are found\" need a browser. It can tell that a chart has neither `title` nor `labelledBy`, but not whether a `labelledBy` id exists.\n\n## Every code\n\n[table: diagnostic codes](../data/diagnostics.mjs)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
